2292
03:03:31,872 --> 03:03:33,733
why that made her so uneasy.

2293
03:03:35,804 --> 03:03:41,255
The first call came two weeks later, at twenty to
midnight, from a number she did not

2294
03:03:41,275 --> 03:03:41,898
recognise.

2295
03:03:42,588 --> 03:03:44,139
Is this Hannah Price?

2296
03:03:44,660 --> 03:03:45,908
Who's asking?

2297
03:03:46,427 --> 03:03:51,927
Someone who reads your work. I'm calling from a phone
I bought with cash at a gas station

2298
03:03:51,947 --> 03:03:57,322
in Newark. I'm going to throw it in the river when
I'm done. Don't try to call it back.

2299
03:03:58,641 --> 03:04:04,038
She knew his voice. She did not know how she knew it
— she had never spoken to him — but

2300
03:04:04,058 --> 03:04:09,454
it was the voice of an educated man in his late
fifties, precise, frightened, and trying

2301
03:04:09,474 --> 03:04:14,685
very hard to sound as though he was not. She had
heard a great many voices like it on

2302
03:04:14,705 --> 03:04:19,978
a great many late-night calls. They were the voices
of people who had worked somewhere

2303
03:04:19,998 --> 03:04:24,841
for a long time, and seen something, and been
unable to stop thinking about it.

2304
03:04:25,531 --> 03:04:26,699
I'm listening.

2305
03:04:27,218 --> 03:04:31,443
You're writing about him. The profile.
Everybody knows. He knows.

2306
03:04:31,963 --> 03:04:33,594
That isn't a secret.

2307
03:04:34,114 --> 03:04:39,229
No. Nothing is, with him. That's what I want to tell
you. Whatever you think you're seeing,

2308
03:04:39,249 --> 03:04:41,036
he's already seen you seeing it.

2309
03:04:42,557 --> 03:04:43,965
What do you want to tell me?

2310
03:04:45,983 --> 03:04:47,957
Ask him about Control Seven.

2311
03:04:48,478 --> 03:04:50,115
What's Control Seven?

2312
03:04:50,635 --> 03:04:56,107
Ask him. Watch his face when you ask. And then —
then ask yourself why a man who wants

2313
03:04:56,127 --> 03:05:00,322
the whole world to watch him never, ever
lets anyone see his eyes.

2314
03:05:00,841 --> 03:05:02,183
The line went dead.

2315
03:05:02,753 --> 03:05:08,273
In the parking lot of St. Anthony's church, Howard
Lessing sat for a long time with the

2316
03:05:08,293 --> 03:05:14,066
phone in his lap, shaking. Then he drove to the river,
and threw it in, as he had promised.

2317
03:05:14,086 --> 03:05:19,796
He drove home the long way, under the speed limit,
checking his mirrors every few seconds,

2318
03:05:19,816 --> 03:05:25,335
and when he got home he sat in his car in the garage
for twenty minutes before he could

2319
03:05:25,355 --> 03:05:27,054
bring himself to go inside.

2320
03:05:28,626 --> 03:05:34,716
It had been nearly two years since the night in lab
two. The fear had not gone away. It

2321
03:05:34,736 --> 03:05:41,037
had simply, very slowly, begun to decay — the way the
Adolf half of the mind had predicted

2322
03:05:41,057 --> 03:05:47,147
it would — and in the space it left behind,
something older had begun to grow back. Not

2323
03:05:47,167 --> 03:05:50,237
courage, exactly. Something
closer to spite.

2324
03:05:50,807 --> 03:05:56,624
He had been careful. He had used cash. He had driven
to a church in a parish he did not

2325
03:05:56,644 --> 03:06:01,991
belong to, in another county, and he had left his
own phone at home in a drawer.

2326
03:06:02,562 --> 03:06:08,051
For the first time since Albert Wesker had arrived in
the world, somebody had done something

2327
03:06:08,071 --> 03:06:09,368
that Hale did not see.

2328
03:06:11,438 --> 03:06:13,456
She found the second thread herself.

2329
03:06:14,026 --> 03:06:20,232
It had taken her four months, working backward from a
line in a court filing that nobody

2330
03:06:20,252 --> 03:06:26,457
else had read — a bill from a private investigation
firm, submitted by Graham Tolliver's

2331
03:06:26,477 --> 03:06:32,540
lawyers in the Chancery case and then quietly
withdrawn. Two retired Wilmington police

2332
03:06:32,560 --> 03:06:38,765
officers. One was now in Arizona, and hung up the
moment he heard the word Umbrella. The

2333
03:06:38,785 --> 03:06:45,061
other was a deacon at a small church in Lancaster
County, and he did not hang up, because

2334
03:06:45,081 --> 03:06:50,861
he was a Christian, and he did not believe in
being rude to women on his doorstep.

2335
03:06:51,551 --> 03:06:55,154
I won't talk about it, miss. I'm sorry
you came all this way.

2336
03:06:55,675 --> 03:06:59,966
I'm not asking you to talk about it, Mister Dorsey.
I'm only asking you one thing. What

2337
03:06:59,986 --> 03:07:01,354
did you see in the basement?

2338
03:07:03,074 --> 03:07:09,057
He did not answer for a long time. A car went by on
the road, and a dog barked somewhere.

2339
03:07:09,747 --> 03:07:14,423
There were two men. They didn't blink. The whole
time. I was a cop for twenty-six years,

2340
03:07:14,443 --> 03:07:19,280
miss, and I've seen men on every drug there is, and
I've never seen a man who didn't blink.

2341
03:07:19,800 --> 03:07:20,622
And?

2342
03:07:21,141 --> 03:07:25,782
And there was a machine in the corner. A big grey
thing, like an air conditioner. And

2343
03:07:25,802 --> 03:07:26,441
it breathed.

2344
03:07:27,841 --> 03:07:32,479
It breathed, miss. Like something asleep. And there
was a window in it. And something

2345
03:07:32,499 --> 03:07:33,795
in there opened its eye.

2346
03:07:35,814 --> 03:07:41,219
He went inside and closed the door. She stood on the
step for a while, in the sunshine,

2347
03:07:41,239 --> 03:07:44,275
with her notebook open and
nothing written in it.

2348
03:07:44,845 --> 03:07:50,654
On the drive home she told herself that he was a
traumatised man, and that trauma distorts

2349
03:07:50,674 --> 03:07:56,419
memory, and that a compressor cycle can sound like
breathing if you are frightened enough

2350
03:07:56,439 --> 03:08:00,111
in the dark. All of which was true.
None of which helped.

2351
03:08:02,180 --> 03:08:06,100
The Director's invitation came
through Tomás, in October.

2352
03:08:06,670 --> 03:08:12,962
Full access. That was the offer. Six weeks. Any floor
of the tower, any meeting she wanted

2353
03:08:12,982 --> 03:08:19,135
to sit in on, any employee she wanted to interview,
and six hours of the Director's time

2354
03:08:19,155 --> 03:08:22,923
in person, spread across the six
weeks, on the record.

2355
03:08:23,492 --> 03:08:29,335
She knew exactly what it was. It was the clean face
— every floor he wanted her to see,

2356
03:08:29,355 --> 03:08:35,131
every person he wanted her to meet. She knew that
the access was the cage. She took it

2357
03:08:35,151 --> 03:08:41,330
anyway, because access is also a door, and she intended
to walk through it and keep walking.

2358
03:08:41,901 --> 03:08:47,886
She saw everything they showed her. She saw the assay
programme on the ninth floor, where

2359
03:08:47,906 --> 03:08:53,824
the second-generation device — smaller, faster,
forty-six pathogens now instead of forty

2360
03:08:53,844 --> 03:08:59,896
— was being prepared for launch. She saw the Aeterna
trial ward, and met Arthur Kowalczyk,

2361
03:08:59,916 --> 03:09:06,037
eighty years old now, who shook her hand with a grip
that hurt and told her he was learning

2362
03:09:06,057 --> 03:09:07,454
to ride a motorcycle.

2363
03:09:08,024 --> 03:09:13,528
She saw Evelyn Marsh, who gave her forty minutes and
said nothing at all, beautifully.

2364
03:09:14,098 --> 03:09:20,169
She saw Catherine Adeyemi, who gave her an hour and
said a great deal, all of it on the

2365
03:09:20,189 --> 03:09:23,390
record, all of it true, all of
it about money.

2366
03:09:24,959 --> 03:09:26,692
And she saw the men in black.

2367
03:09:27,262 --> 03:09:33,150
She tried to photograph one of them, on the third day,
in a corridor on the eleventh floor

2368
03:09:33,170 --> 03:09:39,189
— a tall man with short hair standing outside the
Director's office. She took four pictures.

2369
03:09:39,209 --> 03:09:44,769
In all four, his face was smeared, as if he had
moved, although she had been watching

2370
03:09:44,789 --> 03:09:46,870
him and he had not moved at all.

2371
03:09:47,439 --> 03:09:53,103
She showed Tomás. Tomás laughed and said the
building had a lot of glass and the light

2372
03:09:53,123 --> 03:09:58,523
was weird. He said it too quickly. She wrote down
that he had said it too quickly.

2373
03:10:00,593 --> 03:10:02,966
The last of the six hours was a dinner.

2374
03:10:03,536 --> 03:10:09,332
He chose the restaurant — a quiet place in
Philadelphia with dark wood and a private room

2375
03:10:09,352 --> 03:10:14,952
— and he ordered for both of them again, and again
it was what she would have ordered,

2376
03:10:14,972 --> 03:10:16,651
and this time she said so.

2377
03:10:17,339 --> 03:10:18,995
You do that on purpose.

2378
03:10:19,516 --> 03:10:22,568
I do everything on purpose. It's a
character flaw.

2379
03:10:23,088 --> 03:10:24,744
How do you know what I'd order?

2380
03:10:25,264 --> 03:10:30,492
You told me. The way you read the menu. You looked at
the fish twice and the steak once,

2381
03:10:30,512 --> 03:10:35,621
and your eyes went back to the fish, and you have an
espresso habit, so you won't want

2382
03:10:35,641 --> 03:10:39,080
a heavy sauce. It isn't magic, Miss
Price. It's attention.

2383
03:10:40,600 --> 03:10:45,068
The recorder was on the table between them. He
had asked her to put it there.

2384
03:10:45,637 --> 03:10:51,264
They talked for two hours. She asked him about
Verdant, and about Aeterna, and about the

2385
03:10:51,284 --> 03:10:57,041
eleven seconds, and he answered every question —
calmly, fluently, charmingly, and without

2386
03:10:57,061 --> 03:11:02,496
telling her a single thing she did not already
know. She had interviewed senators who

2387
03:11:02,516 --> 03:11:06,925
were worse at it. She had interviewed a
cardinal who was worse at it.

2388
03:11:07,495 --> 03:11:13,058
At the end of the second hour, over coffee, she did
what the voice on the phone had told

2389
03:11:13,078 --> 03:11:13,693
her to do.

2390
03:11:15,382 --> 03:11:17,018
What's Control Seven?

2391
03:11:19,039 --> 03:11:25,133
He did not answer immediately. She was watching his
face as closely as she had ever watched

2392
03:11:25,153 --> 03:11:27,956
anything, exactly as she had been told to.

2393
03:11:28,526 --> 03:11:34,354
She saw nothing. That was what she would write in
her notes afterwards. Nothing at all.

2394
03:11:34,374 --> 03:11:40,338
Not a flicker, not a pause in his breathing, not a
movement of his hands. The face across

2395
03:11:40,358 --> 03:11:43,834
the table did not change by so much
as a millimetre.

2396
03:11:44,404 --> 03:11:50,110
It was, she realised later, the most frightening
thing she had ever seen. Because every

2397
03:11:50,130 --> 03:11:55,704
human face changes when it is surprised. And his
had not — which could only mean that

2398
03:11:55,724 --> 03:12:01,694
he had not been surprised. Which could only mean that
he had known the question was coming.

2399
03:12:03,883 --> 03:12:09,423
It's a calibration sample. Howard Lessing ran it in
the autumn of twenty twenty-six, and

2400
03:12:09,443 --> 03:12:14,730
it broke an analyzer, and he's never forgiven me
for being right about the reagents.

2401
03:12:16,050 --> 03:12:19,079
He had said Howard Lessing's
name. She had not.

2402
03:12:19,650 --> 03:12:24,938
She understood, sitting in the candlelight with the
rain on the tall windows, that he

2403
03:12:24,958 --> 03:12:30,496
had just told her he knew exactly who had called her.
And she understood, a second later,

2404
03:12:30,516 --> 03:12:32,995
that he had wanted her to understand it.

2405
03:12:35,185 --> 03:12:36,376
Who are you?

2406
03:12:36,896 --> 03:12:42,101
It was not a journalist's question. It came out of
her before she could stop it, in a

2407
03:12:42,121 --> 03:12:44,991
different voice than the one she
used for work.

2408
03:12:46,681 --> 03:12:51,867
I'll tell you one true thing, Hannah. Just one. And
then you can write whatever you like.

2409
03:12:53,066 --> 03:12:58,571
I was born in September twenty twenty-six.
Everything before that is paperwork.

2410
03:13:00,592 --> 03:13:06,483
She wrote it down. Of course she wrote it down. It was
the best line in the whole profile,

2411
03:13:06,503 --> 03:13:12,197
and she used it as the last line, and when the
magazine came out, everybody who read it

2412
03:13:12,217 --> 03:13:17,780
took it the way she had taken it at the table: as a
metaphor. A self-made man's boast

2413
03:13:17,800 --> 03:13:23,495
about reinvention. The kind of thing a charismatic
chief executive says to a journalist

2414
03:13:23,515 --> 03:13:27,238
over coffee, because he knows it will
look good in print.

2415
03:13:27,809 --> 03:13:31,741
It was the only completely true thing he
told her in six weeks.

2416
03:13:33,810 --> 03:13:35,876
She got home at twenty past one.

2417
03:13:36,444 --> 03:13:41,999
The apartment was exactly as she had left it. The
door had been locked. The window had

2418
03:13:42,019 --> 03:13:45,758
been latched. The cat was on the bed.
Nothing was missing.

2419
03:13:46,329 --> 03:13:50,598
Her notebook was on her desk, where she had
left it, beside the laptop.

2420
03:13:51,167 --> 03:13:54,079
It was one inch to the left of
where she had left it.

2421
03:13:54,650 --> 03:14:00,027
She knew it was one inch because she had a habit,
from her years on the courts beat, of

2422
03:14:00,047 --> 03:14:05,548
squaring her notebook against the edge of the desk
when she left it, and it was no longer

2423
03:14:05,568 --> 03:14:11,132
square. It had been moved. Carefully, and not
carefully enough. Or — she thought, standing

2424
03:14:11,152 --> 03:14:16,715
in the doorway with her keys in her hand and her heart
going very fast — exactly carefully

2425
03:14:16,735 --> 03:14:17,150
enough.

2426
03:14:19,220 --> 03:14:24,696
She did not call the police. There was nothing to
report. A notebook, moved one inch.

2427
03:14:25,266 --> 03:14:30,110
She sat up for the rest of the night at her kitchen
table with the lights on and the cat

2428
03:14:30,130 --> 03:14:34,808
in her lap, and she understood something that would
stay with her for the rest of her

2429
03:14:34,828 --> 03:14:39,560
career: that for two years she had been following a
story, and for two years the story

2430
03:14:39,580 --> 03:14:44,258
had been following her back. That it had smelled
her the way she had smelled it. That

2431
03:14:44,278 --> 03:14:49,177
it knew her rent, and her ex-boyfriend's name, and
the fact that she squared her notebook

2432
03:14:49,197 --> 03:14:50,780
against the edge of the desk.

2433
03:14:51,349 --> 03:14:53,320
And that it had wanted her to know.

2434
03:14:55,390 --> 03:15:01,123
The profile ran in January. It was twelve thousand
words long. It was, everyone agreed,

2435
03:15:01,143 --> 03:15:06,942
the best thing ever written about Albert Wesker. It
was fair, and sharp, and beautifully

2436
03:15:06,962 --> 03:15:12,563
written, and it said that he was the most
charismatic chief executive in America, and

2437
03:15:12,583 --> 03:15:16,597
that he was hiding something, and that
she did not know what.

2438
03:15:17,166 --> 03:15:22,361
It did not mention Control Seven. It did not
mention a machine that breathed.

2439
03:15:22,930 --> 03:15:27,401
She told her editor she did not have enough to
stand it up. That was true.

2440
03:15:43,456 --> 03:15:49,884
Nadia Okafor did not decide to become a whistleblower.
Nobody ever does. She simply noticed

2441
03:15:49,903 --> 03:15:56,543
things, one at a time, and wrote them down, and one
night in the winter of twenty twenty-eight

2442
03:15:56,563 --> 03:16:02,637
she opened the folder on her phone called Recipes
and realised that it was no longer a

2443
03:16:02,657 --> 03:16:04,195
folder. It was a case.

2444
03:16:05,765 --> 03:16:08,983
The manifest had been the first
thing. There were others.

2445
03:16:09,554 --> 03:16:15,771
There was Deshawn at the loading dock, who liked her
because she remembered his daughter's

2446
03:16:15,791 --> 03:16:21,662
birthday, and who mentioned, one evening, that the
old building across the river took

2447
03:16:21,682 --> 03:16:27,623
a delivery of liquid nitrogen every nine days.
Enough, Nadia calculated in her head as

2448
03:16:27,643 --> 03:16:33,653
he said it, to keep about four hundred litres of
material at cryogenic temperature — or

2449
03:16:33,673 --> 03:16:39,545
to run a cold room the size of a garage. The old
building's basement had six freezers

2450
03:16:39,564 --> 03:16:45,851
from the nineteen-nineties and a vault Pell had built
in the spring. None of it used liquid

2451
03:16:45,871 --> 03:16:46,475
nitrogen.

2452
03:16:47,045 --> 03:16:53,147
There was the electricity. Umbrella's facilities team
published a sustainability report,

2453
03:16:53,167 --> 03:16:59,547
because Catherine had insisted on one, and the report
listed the power use of each building.

2454
03:16:59,567 --> 03:17:05,600
The old building on the south bank — four floors, a
basement, a few dozen people — drew

2455
03:17:05,620 --> 03:17:08,869
more power than the entire
twelve-storey tower.

2456
03:17:09,440 --> 03:17:15,366
And there was Evelyn. Nadia had watched her, twice,
from the car park of the old building

2457
03:17:15,386 --> 03:17:21,313
at night, step into the freight elevator in the
basement with a sealed case, and go down.

2458
03:17:21,332 --> 03:17:27,125
The freight elevator went to the basement and the
loading dock. It did not go down. And

2459
03:17:27,145 --> 03:17:31,200
both times, Evelyn Marsh had not come
back up for nine hours.

2460
03:17:33,270 --> 03:17:39,002
None of it was proof. All of it was a smell. And
Nadia Okafor had spent her whole career

2461
03:17:39,022 --> 03:17:43,316
learning to trust a smell before she could
see what was making it.

2462
03:17:45,385 --> 03:17:50,909
The proof, when it came, came because Evelyn Marsh
had stopped thinking of other people

2463
03:17:50,929 --> 03:17:51,546
as a risk.

2464
03:17:52,117 --> 03:17:58,043
It was a Thursday in February. Evelyn had come up from
below to sign off the second-generation

2465
03:17:58,063 --> 03:18:03,484
assay, which was still, officially, her programme,
and she had been called away in the

2466
03:18:03,504 --> 03:18:09,051
middle of it by one of the research associates, who
had appeared in the doorway and said

2467
03:18:09,072 --> 03:18:14,429
something too quietly for anyone to hear. She had
gone with him at once. She had left

2468
03:18:14,448 --> 03:18:15,819
a binder on the bench.

2469
03:18:16,390 --> 03:18:22,264
It was a black binder. It had no title on the spine.
Nadia, walking past on her way to

2470
03:18:22,284 --> 03:18:28,228
the lifts, glanced at it, and stopped, and — with
the terrible slowness of a person who

2471
03:18:28,248 --> 03:18:33,095
knows she is about to do something that
cannot be undone — turned back.

2472
03:18:34,664 --> 03:18:37,220
The first page she saw was a photograph.

2473
03:18:37,791 --> 03:18:44,227
It had been taken through thick glass, in cold light.
It showed a cryogenic capsule, very

2474
03:18:44,247 --> 03:18:50,465
large, and inside it, in a pale mist, a figure. The
figure was grey, and hairless, and

2475
03:18:50,485 --> 03:18:56,703
far too big. Beneath the photograph, in Evelyn's
neat, small handwriting, was a label.

2476
03:18:56,723 --> 03:19:00,619
T-zero-zero. Subject. Week
eighty-one. Vitals nominal.

2477
03:19:02,689 --> 03:19:08,943
Nadia looked at it for a long time. She told herself it
was a model. A sculpture. A prosthetic

2478
03:19:08,963 --> 03:19:14,682
from a film studio. She told herself that for about
four seconds, which was as long as

2479
03:19:14,702 --> 03:19:16,350
her training would allow.

2480
03:19:16,920 --> 03:19:18,384
Then she turned the page.

2481
03:19:18,954 --> 03:19:25,503
It was a table. Twenty-four subjects. Rhesus macaques
— the twenty-four from the Singapore

2482
03:19:25,523 --> 03:19:31,781
manifest, she understood at once, with a lurch in
her stomach. Each one had been given

2483
03:19:31,801 --> 03:19:38,351
a compound labelled P-strain derivative, in rising
doses. The column on the right recorded

2484
03:19:38,371 --> 03:19:39,009
outcomes.

2485
03:19:39,579 --> 03:19:41,421
Nineteen of them said deceased.

2486
03:19:41,992 --> 03:19:43,700
Five of them said enhanced.

2487
03:19:45,469 --> 03:19:51,521
Beneath the table, in the margin, in the same small
neat hand, Evelyn had written a single

2488
03:19:51,541 --> 03:19:54,287
note to herself, and underlined it twice.

2489
03:19:54,855 --> 03:19:59,295
Tolerance correlates with baseline
aggression. Select accordingly.

2490
03:20:01,365 --> 03:20:06,788
She photographed six pages. Her hands were shaking
so badly that she had to take two of

2491
03:20:06,808 --> 03:20:12,231
them twice. Then she closed the binder, and squared
it exactly where it had been on the

2492
03:20:12,251 --> 03:20:17,549
bench, and walked to the lifts, and pressed the
button, and stood very still, and did

2493
03:20:17,569 --> 03:20:19,989
not breathe until the doors had closed.

2494
03:20:20,559 --> 03:20:26,197
Evelyn came back fourteen minutes later, and picked
up the binder, and did not notice

2495
03:20:26,217 --> 03:20:27,263
anything at all.

2496
03:20:29,331 --> 03:20:31,672
She did not sleep that night, or the next.

2497
03:20:32,242 --> 03:20:37,541
She thought about her mother in Lagos, whose
operation Umbrella's salary had paid for.

2498
03:20:37,561 --> 03:20:42,921
She thought about her niece in Manchester, in the
second year of a medical degree Nadia

2499
03:20:42,941 --> 03:20:48,240
was funding. She thought about Arthur Kowalczyk on
his motorcycle, and about the three

2500
03:20:48,260 --> 03:20:53,682
hundred people now in the Aeterna trials whose lives
were getting better every week, and

2501
03:20:53,702 --> 03:20:59,063
about what would happen to all of them if Umbrella
Life Sciences became, overnight, the

2502
03:20:59,082 --> 03:21:01,227
subject of a federal investigation.

2503
03:21:01,797 --> 03:21:07,398
And she thought about nineteen monkeys, and a note
in a margin. Select accordingly.

2504
03:21:08,968 --> 03:21:14,313
At five in the morning, on the third night, she
opened her laptop and began to write.

2505
03:21:16,381 --> 03:21:18,125
The Director knew by Monday.

2506
03:21:18,697 --> 03:21:24,639
He knew it the way he knew most things now — across
a table, in the first ten seconds

2507
03:21:24,659 --> 03:21:30,602
of a meeting. Nadia Okafor sat in her usual place,
and said good morning in her usual

2508
03:21:30,622 --> 03:21:35,514
voice, and was, to every other person at the
table, perfectly herself.

2509
03:21:36,083 --> 03:21:41,930
To the Cold-Read she was a flare in the dark. Fear,
bright and white. Grief — a great

2510
03:21:41,950 --> 03:21:48,072
deal of grief, which surprised him. And beneath it,
like bedrock, something the Cold-Read

2511
03:21:48,092 --> 03:21:54,422
rarely showed him in anyone: a decision that had
already been made, and would not be unmade.

2512
03:21:56,112 --> 03:21:58,005
She is going to betray you.

2513
03:21:58,527 --> 03:22:01,023
She's going to leave. It isn't
the same thing.

2514
03:22:01,543 --> 03:22:05,960
She has seen something. She will take it
to your enemies. Stop her.

2515
03:22:06,480 --> 03:22:07,036
How?

2516
03:22:07,555 --> 03:22:08,902
You know how.

2517
03:22:09,422 --> 03:22:13,711
Say it, then. Say it out loud, since you've
got nothing but time in there.

2518
03:22:15,230 --> 03:22:23,250
She has a mother in Lagos. And a niece in Manchester.
People with families can be persuaded

2519
03:22:23,269 --> 03:22:25,635
to remember their families.

2520
03:22:27,655 --> 03:22:34,194
The Director did not answer that for the rest of the
meeting. He discussed the assay launch,

2521
03:22:34,215 --> 03:22:40,397
and the Aeterna phase three enrolment, and a new
facility in Singapore. He did not look

2522
03:22:40,417 --> 03:22:41,895
at Nadia Okafor once.

2523
03:22:42,466 --> 03:22:48,371
But in the quiet of one skull, for forty minutes, a
very old argument was taking place,

2524
03:22:48,391 --> 03:22:52,731
and it was not the argument either of
them had expected to have.

2525
03:22:54,620 --> 03:22:56,774
We are not going to threaten her family.

2526
03:22:57,293 --> 03:23:02,875
Why not? You are not squeamish. I have been inside
your head for two years. You are the

2527
03:23:02,895 --> 03:23:07,897
least squeamish man I have ever met, and I have
met — I have met a great many.

2528
03:23:08,418 --> 03:23:13,981
It isn't squeamishness. It's arithmetic. If I
threaten her family, she becomes a martyr

2529
03:23:14,001 --> 03:23:20,143
with a motive. If I let her go, she becomes a disgruntled
former employee with some photographs.

2530
03:23:20,162 --> 03:23:24,186
One of those makes the evening news. The
other makes page nine.

2531
03:23:24,706 --> 03:23:26,265
And the photographs?

2532
03:23:26,786 --> 03:23:32,269
Show a large grey sculpture and a table of numbers
in a binder. The FBI will come. The

2533
03:23:32,289 --> 03:23:37,709
CDC will come. They'll come to a clean company with
a spotless inspection history and

2534
03:23:37,729 --> 03:23:43,149
a drug that makes old men walk. And they'll find
nothing, because what they'd need to

2535
03:23:43,170 --> 03:23:45,389
find isn't anywhere they can reach.

2536
03:23:46,908 --> 03:23:48,672
You want them to come.

2537
03:23:49,192 --> 03:23:54,473
I want them to come now. While I choose the ground.
A controlled burn, Adolf. You set

2538
03:23:54,493 --> 03:23:58,527
a small fire on purpose, so there's
nothing left for the big one.

2539
03:24:00,045 --> 03:24:02,117
And if you are wrong?

2540
03:24:02,636 --> 03:24:08,031
Then I'll have learned something about this world.
That's worth more than one virologist.

2541
03:24:10,051 --> 03:24:15,343
She came to resign in person. She could have sent
an email. She could have walked out

2542
03:24:15,363 --> 03:24:20,904
on a Friday night and never come back. She came to
his office instead, at half past four,

2543
03:24:20,924 --> 03:24:26,340
and knocked, because she was a person who knocked,
and she handed him a single sheet of

2544
03:24:26,360 --> 03:24:26,715
paper.

2545
03:24:27,405 --> 03:24:32,822
I'm leaving, Albert. Effective today. I'm sorry for the
short notice. I think you'll understand

2546
03:24:32,842 --> 03:24:33,050
why.

2547
03:24:33,569 --> 03:24:34,275
I do.

2548
03:24:35,795 --> 03:24:41,255
She had expected him to ask. She had rehearsed the
conversation in the car. She had rehearsed

2549
03:24:41,275 --> 03:24:46,559
what she would say when he asked what she had seen,
and what she would say when he offered

2550
03:24:46,579 --> 03:24:51,687
her money, and — though she had not let herself
think about it clearly — what she would

2551
03:24:51,707 --> 03:24:53,455
say if he did something worse.

2552
03:24:54,027 --> 03:24:59,061
He did none of it. He simply looked at the letter,
and then at her, and then took off

2553
03:24:59,081 --> 03:25:01,439
his sunglasses and set them on the desk.

2554
03:25:02,009 --> 03:25:07,977
His eyes were ordinary. Pale, and grey, and very
tired, in a way she had never seen before.

2555
03:25:07,997 --> 03:25:13,834
She would think about that for years. She would
wonder whether he had done it on purpose.

2556
03:25:14,525 --> 03:25:20,001
You're going to go to the CDC. And the FDA.
And they'll pass it to the FBI.

2557
03:25:20,520 --> 03:25:21,115
Yes.

2558
03:25:21,635 --> 03:25:26,493
They'll come. They'll come here, to this building,
with warrants and badges, and they'll

2559
03:25:26,513 --> 03:25:31,483
find a clean company with a spotless record, and a
drug that makes old men walk, and three

2560
03:25:31,503 --> 03:25:36,417
hundred patients who'll tell them it saved their
lives. And you'll spend the rest of your

2561
03:25:36,437 --> 03:25:39,300
career being the woman who was
wrong about Umbrella.

2562
03:25:41,020 --> 03:25:42,034
Then I'll be wrong.

2563
03:25:44,054 --> 03:25:49,664
He looked at her for a long time. The Cold-Read
showed him exactly what she felt, and

2564
03:25:49,684 --> 03:25:55,626
it was the one thing, in two years, that he had not
been able to make anyone in this world

2565
03:25:55,646 --> 03:26:01,057
feel toward him, not with money, not with fear,
not with a speech in a break room.

2566
03:26:01,627 --> 03:26:04,694
She was not afraid of him. She
was sorry for him.

2567
03:26:06,385 --> 03:26:07,474
Goodbye, Nadia.

2568
03:26:07,993 --> 03:26:08,997
Goodbye, Albert.

2569
03:26:10,518 --> 03:26:15,902
She took her phone with her. He did not ask for it.
She walked out of the office, and

2570
03:26:15,921 --> 03:26:21,432
down the corridor, and past Hale, who stood by the
lifts with his hands folded in front

2571
03:26:21,452 --> 03:26:26,327
of him and watched her go, and did not move,
because he had not been told to.

2572
03:26:28,396 --> 03:26:30,564
Dana Whitfield caught her in the lobby.

2573
03:26:31,135 --> 03:26:37,273
Nobody had told Dana. Nobody needed to. She had worked
with Nadia Okafor for twelve years,

2574
03:26:37,293 --> 03:26:43,225
and she saw her face, and the box of things from her
desk, and she came out from behind

2575
03:26:43,245 --> 03:26:47,947
the reception desk and hugged her, hard, in
front of the living wall.

2576
03:26:48,636 --> 03:26:49,548
Be careful.

2577
03:26:50,068 --> 03:26:50,871
You too.

2578
03:26:52,391 --> 03:26:58,025
Dana let her go. She stood in the lobby for a long
time after the doors had closed, looking

2579
03:26:58,045 --> 03:27:03,492
out at the rain. She did not know what Nadia had
seen. She did not want to know. But she

2580
03:27:03,512 --> 03:27:09,145
had noticed, for the first time that week, that she
had started saying Be careful to people

2581
03:27:09,165 --> 03:27:12,872
who were leaving the building, instead
of Have a good night.

2582
03:27:13,443 --> 03:27:17,556
She did not know when that had started. She
thought it had been a while.

2583
03:27:19,626 --> 03:27:22,608
Nadia Okafor walked out into the rain.

2584
03:27:23,176 --> 03:27:28,965
She did not open an umbrella. She had one in her
bag. She would say afterwards that she

2585
03:27:28,985 --> 03:27:34,974
simply did not want to, that night, and she would not
be able to explain why, and everyone

2586
03:27:34,994 --> 03:27:36,109
would understand.

2587
03:27:38,680 --> 03:27:44,501
The complaint reached the Centers for Disease Control
and Prevention in Atlanta nineteen

2588
03:27:44,521 --> 03:27:50,409
days later, through a portal most people did not know
existed, and was assigned — because

2589
03:27:50,429 --> 03:27:56,184
she was the most junior officer on the relevant
desk, and because nobody else wanted it

2590
03:27:56,204 --> 03:28:01,827
— to a thirty-nine-year-old epidemic intelligence
officer named Doctor Samira Haddad.

2591
03:28:02,396 --> 03:28:07,402
She read it at her desk at six in the evening,
with a sandwich she forgot to eat.

2592
03:28:07,972 --> 03:28:13,387
She looked at the photograph of the grey figure
first, and she laughed out loud, once,

2593
03:28:13,407 --> 03:28:15,411
and said to nobody: oh, come on.

2594
03:28:15,979 --> 03:28:22,403
Then she looked at the table. Twenty-four macaques.
A compound with no registered name.

2595
03:28:22,423 --> 03:28:29,439
Nineteen deceased. Five enhanced. And a note in a
margin, in small neat handwriting, underlined

2596
03:28:29,459 --> 03:28:29,884
twice.

2597
03:28:30,454 --> 03:28:31,761
She stopped laughing.

2598
03:28:33,330 --> 03:28:38,904
Samira Haddad had spent four years chasing outbreaks
in places where nobody else wanted

2599
03:28:38,924 --> 03:28:44,884
to go. She had learned that most complaints are
nothing, and that the ones that are something

2600
03:28:44,904 --> 03:28:50,478
rarely look like it at first, and that the most
dangerous words in her profession were:

2601
03:28:50,498 --> 03:28:51,764
that can't be right.

2602
03:28:52,333 --> 03:28:57,590
She read the complaint twice more. Then she picked
up the phone, and called a friend at

2603
03:28:57,609 --> 03:29:00,197
the Food and Drug
Administration, and said:

2604
03:29:17,198 --> 03:29:22,650
In the spring of twenty twenty-nine, while a
virologist's complaint made its slow way

2605
03:29:22,671 --> 03:29:27,930
through the inboxes of the federal government,
Umbrella Life Sciences went abroad.

2606
03:29:28,501 --> 03:29:34,064
It went in three directions at once, and each
direction had a reason, and only one of

2607
03:29:34,083 --> 03:29:36,297
the reasons was ever written down.

2608
03:29:37,867 --> 03:29:44,070
The first was England. It was, officially, a matter
of talent — Cambridge had more good

2609
03:29:44,090 --> 03:29:50,293
biologists per square mile than anywhere on Earth.
It was also a matter of history. The

2610
03:29:50,314 --> 03:29:57,018
last remaining asset of the dead parent company,
Umbrella Holdings, was a shuttered laboratory

2611
03:29:57,037 --> 03:30:03,242
on the edge of Cambridge that Harrow and Lisle had
never managed to sell. Peter Fenwick

2612
03:30:03,261 --> 03:30:09,394
sold it to the Director over the phone, for less
than its value in scrap, and said, as

2613
03:30:09,413 --> 03:30:12,611
the call ended, in a tone of
mild bafflement:

2614
03:30:13,300 --> 03:30:17,865
Do you know, Doctor Wesker, you're the only man who's
ever bought anything from this estate

2615
03:30:17,885 --> 03:30:18,168
twice?

2616
03:30:18,689 --> 03:30:20,461
I'm sentimental, Mister Fenwick.

2617
03:30:21,780 --> 03:30:28,100
Peter Fenwick laughed. It was the last time anyone at
Harrow and Lisle thought about Umbrella

2618
03:30:28,120 --> 03:30:29,327
for sixteen years.

2619
03:30:31,099 --> 03:30:37,006
The second was Switzerland. Officially, a sample
repository — a cold vault in the rock

2620
03:30:37,027 --> 03:30:43,143
above Basel, where Umbrella could store biological
material under the protection of Swiss

2621
03:30:43,162 --> 03:30:49,416
law and Swiss discretion. It was also, though nobody
but Pell knew this, the first property

2622
03:30:49,436 --> 03:30:55,759
Umbrella had ever bought in a city that the Adolf half
of the mind had marked, years before,

2623
03:30:55,778 --> 03:30:57,551
on a map inside the skull.

2624
03:30:58,120 --> 03:31:04,452
Basel. The pharmaceutical capital of the world. Home
of the giants. A city where a single

2625
03:31:04,472 --> 03:31:10,948
industry paid for everything, and where a single
company could, in the right circumstances,

2626
03:31:10,967 --> 03:31:13,802
become indistinguishable from the state.

2627
03:31:14,492 --> 03:31:15,568
One day.

2628
03:31:16,087 --> 03:31:17,448
One day. Not this year.

2629
03:31:19,168 --> 03:31:25,257
And the third was Singapore, where Ashford Pacific
had quietly become one of the larger

2630
03:31:25,278 --> 03:31:31,227
logistics firms in Southeast Asia, and where
Umbrella now built — with its own money,

2631
03:31:31,247 --> 03:31:37,618
in the open, with every permit in order — a
manufacturing plant that could produce Aeterna,

2632
03:31:37,638 --> 03:31:43,728
and the assay, and anything else the future
required, at a rate of four hundred million

2633
03:31:43,748 --> 03:31:44,640
doses a year.

2634
03:31:45,211 --> 03:31:51,097
Four hundred million. Catherine asked, in the
capital planning meeting, why a company

2635
03:31:51,117 --> 03:31:57,073
with a drug still in phase three trials needed
capacity for four hundred million doses

2636
03:31:57,093 --> 03:31:57,906
of anything.

2637
03:31:58,476 --> 03:32:03,191
The Director said that he believed in being
prepared. She wrote that down.

2638
03:32:06,012 --> 03:32:11,048
Assessment. Expansion. Three
hundred thousand Points.

2639
03:32:15,896 --> 03:32:18,250
Level up. Level twenty.

2640
03:32:20,382 --> 03:32:26,237
Twenty. He felt this one differently. Not like doors
opening, this time. Like the floor

2641
03:32:26,257 --> 03:32:31,235
of a building dropping away beneath him, and
finding that he did not fall.

2642
03:32:32,555 --> 03:32:33,626
Status.

2643
03:32:38,795 --> 03:32:44,722
Speed fifty. He tested it that night, on an empty
service road behind the plant, at two

2644
03:32:44,742 --> 03:32:50,807
in the morning. He did not run. He simply decided to
be at the far end of the road, three

2645
03:32:50,827 --> 03:32:56,618
hundred metres away, and was — with a sound behind
him like a sheet being torn, and a

2646
03:32:56,638 --> 03:32:59,694
swirl of dust that took a
second to catch up.

2647
03:33:01,815 --> 03:33:05,153
Purchase confirmed. Boardroom gravity.

2648
03:33:11,400 --> 03:33:12,233
Now.

2649
03:33:12,753 --> 03:33:13,534
Now what?

2650
03:33:14,055 --> 03:33:19,657
Now you have two thousand people in four countries.
You have a name that thirteen million

2651
03:33:19,677 --> 03:33:25,028
people have watched. You have a factory for four
hundred million of anything. Now you

2652
03:33:25,047 --> 03:33:27,176
give them something to believe in.

2653
03:33:27,697 --> 03:33:29,198
They believe in their salaries.

2654
03:33:29,719 --> 03:33:32,545
No one ever died for a salary.

2655
03:33:33,064 --> 03:33:38,426
That's rather the point. I don't want anyone to die
for me. Dead people are a terrible

2656
03:33:38,447 --> 03:33:39,115
investment.

2657
03:33:39,635 --> 03:33:45,701
You felt what the break room paid. Four thousand
Points, for six frightened people, in

2658
03:33:45,721 --> 03:33:51,859
five minutes. Imagine what two thousand pay. Imagine
what twenty thousand pay. You want

2659
03:33:51,879 --> 03:33:56,106
the System's money. The System wants
belief. Give it belief.

2660
03:33:57,825 --> 03:34:00,067
Wesker said nothing for a long time.

2661
03:34:00,636 --> 03:34:06,621
He was, underneath everything, an investor. And the
Adolf half had just made — he admitted

2662
03:34:06,641 --> 03:34:12,291
to himself, in the back of the car, with the
streetlights sliding over the glass — an

2663
03:34:12,311 --> 03:34:14,359
extremely good investment case.

2664
03:34:15,049 --> 03:34:15,941
Not a movement.

2665
03:34:16,462 --> 03:34:17,617
A movement.

2666
03:34:18,135 --> 03:34:23,927
A culture. Every great company has one. Apple had
one. Google had one. The Jesuits had

2667
03:34:23,948 --> 03:34:29,941
one, and they ran half the schools in Europe for four
hundred years. Nobody ever arrested

2668
03:34:29,961 --> 03:34:32,644
a Jesuit for having a mission statement.

2669
03:34:33,164 --> 03:34:36,537
Call it what you like. I shall write it.

2670
03:34:38,556 --> 03:34:42,175
They wrote it together, over three
nights, in the corner office.

2671
03:34:42,744 --> 03:34:48,188
The Adolf half wrote the rhythm. It knew exactly how
long a line had to be for a thousand

2672
03:34:48,207 --> 03:34:53,405
people to say it at once without stumbling, and
where the breath should go, and which

2673
03:34:53,424 --> 03:34:58,806
word should land at the end so that the room would
feel it in the chest. The Wesker half

2674
03:34:58,826 --> 03:35:04,085
removed every word that a journalist could ever
quote in a headline. What was left was

2675
03:35:04,104 --> 03:35:09,424
eighty-one words long, and it sounded — on the page
— like the mission statement of any

2676
03:35:09,444 --> 03:35:11,204
ambitious company in America.

2677
03:35:11,775 --> 03:35:16,058
It only sounded like something else when you
heard it said by a crowd.

2678
03:35:17,748 --> 03:35:19,244
It's a cult, Albert.

2679
03:35:19,763 --> 03:35:20,788
It's a culture.

2680
03:35:21,309 --> 03:35:26,153
You've written an oath. You're having new hires
recite it. In unison. At orientation.

2681
03:35:26,672 --> 03:35:27,795
The Army does that.

2682
03:35:28,314 --> 03:35:29,722
We are not the Army.

2683
03:35:30,244 --> 03:35:31,003
Not yet.

2684
03:35:32,323 --> 03:35:38,282
She looked at him sharply. He smiled, as if it were
a joke. It was, the way he said it,

2685
03:35:38,302 --> 03:35:39,587
very nearly a joke.

2686
03:35:41,657 --> 03:35:47,876
The first time it was said aloud was in Singapore, in
June, in an auditorium full of four

2687
03:35:47,896 --> 03:35:51,031
hundred new employees on their
first morning.

2688
03:35:51,600 --> 03:35:57,369
They had been told it was a tradition. They had been
given the words on a card. They stood,

2689
03:35:57,389 --> 03:36:02,840
because the person on the stage asked them to, and
they put their right hands on their

2690
03:36:02,859 --> 03:36:08,246
chests, because the person on the stage did, and
they read the card aloud together, a

2691
03:36:08,266 --> 03:36:13,590
little awkwardly at first, the way people read
anything together for the first time.

2692
03:36:15,161 --> 03:36:20,235
By the second line they had found the rhythm. By
the fourth, they were not reading it

2693
03:36:20,255 --> 03:36:20,716
anymore.

2694
03:36:21,405 --> 03:36:22,664
We are the shelter.

2695
03:36:23,184 --> 03:36:25,494
And four hundred voices said it back.

2696
03:36:26,064 --> 03:36:31,965
We are the shelter. We do not wait for the storm. We
make the world safe for those who

2697
03:36:31,984 --> 03:36:37,814
cannot make it safe for themselves. What we learn,
we keep. What we begin, we finish.

2698
03:36:37,834 --> 03:36:43,872
Where we stand, the rain does not fall. We are not
many. We are enough. We are Umbrella.

2699
03:36:45,941 --> 03:36:51,888
The silence afterwards lasted three seconds. It was
the kind of silence that happens in

2700
03:36:51,908 --> 03:36:56,621
churches, when a hymn ends, and nobody
wants to be the first to move.

2701
03:36:57,191 --> 03:37:02,106
Then they applauded — themselves, and each other,
and the man on the stage — and some

2702
03:37:02,126 --> 03:37:05,707
of them were crying, and none of them
could have told you why.

2703
03:37:08,028 --> 03:37:12,157
Personnel morale, fervent.
Belief, spreading.

2704
03:37:13,746 --> 03:37:19,952
Ninety thousand. For eighty-one words, said by four
hundred people, on a Monday morning

2705
03:37:19,972 --> 03:37:20,884
in Singapore.

2706
03:37:21,573 --> 03:37:23,287
Belief, spreading.

2707
03:37:23,806 --> 03:37:25,131
It's a word on a panel.

2708
03:37:25,650 --> 03:37:31,546
It is the word. I spent my whole life trying to
make that word true. You made it true

2709
03:37:31,565 --> 03:37:33,075
with a laminated card.

2710
03:37:35,095 --> 03:37:41,248
By July, they said it in Wilmington, and in Cambridge,
and in Basel. They said it at every

2711
03:37:41,269 --> 03:37:47,147
orientation and at the start of every quarterly
meeting. It was printed on the wall of

2712
03:37:47,167 --> 03:37:53,046
every lobby, beneath the emblem, in small, elegant
letters that a visitor might easily

2713
03:37:53,065 --> 03:37:55,653
have mistaken for a mission statement.

2714
03:37:56,221 --> 03:38:01,718
Walter Pruitt heard it for the first time at the back
of the Wilmington auditorium, where

2715
03:38:01,738 --> 03:38:07,110
he had been posted for the day because he was
retiring in September and Dana had wanted

2716
03:38:07,130 --> 03:38:07,977
him to see it.

2717
03:38:08,547 --> 03:38:14,365
He stood with his arms folded while three hundred
people said it together. He had said

2718
03:38:14,385 --> 03:38:20,542
an oath like that once, in nineteen eighty, in a
gymnasium at Fort Jackson, South Carolina,

2719
03:38:20,563 --> 03:38:25,497
with his hand on his chest, and it had sent
him, eventually, to a desert.

2720
03:38:26,066 --> 03:38:31,900
He did not say anything to anyone. That evening he
took out the little spiral notebook,

2721
03:38:31,921 --> 03:38:35,938
which was almost full now, and wrote
the date, and one line.

2722
03:38:36,509 --> 03:38:38,447
They're saying it like soldiers.

2723
03:38:40,514 --> 03:38:43,072
Evelyn Marsh said it with her eyes closed.

2724
03:38:43,641 --> 03:38:49,269
Catherine Adeyemi, at the far end of the front row,
moved her lips and did not make a

2725
03:38:49,289 --> 03:38:49,668
sound.

2726
03:38:50,238 --> 03:38:56,468
And the four research associates from the Arklay
sublevel, and the six Silent Ones, and

2727
03:38:56,488 --> 03:39:02,718
Pell, and Gus, and Hale — who had never been told to
attend, and attended anyway — said

2728
03:39:02,738 --> 03:39:09,327
it in perfect unison, with no emphasis on any word,
like a recording, and stopped at exactly

2729
03:39:09,347 --> 03:39:10,549
the same instant.

2730
03:39:11,119 --> 03:39:14,539
Nobody noticed. Everyone was too
busy believing.

2731
03:39:16,609 --> 03:39:19,578
The subpoena arrived on a
Tuesday in August.

2732
03:39:20,148 --> 03:39:25,976
It was served in person, at twelve minutes past nine,
on Dana Whitfield, chief of staff,

2733
03:39:25,996 --> 03:39:31,691
at the front desk of the tower, by a tall man in a
dark suit with a badge on his belt.

2734
03:39:31,711 --> 03:39:37,539
He was polite. He apologised for the inconvenience.
He said the company would be hearing

2735
03:39:37,559 --> 03:39:43,719
from the United States Attorney's office shortly, and
that he hoped everyone would cooperate,

2736
03:39:43,739 --> 03:39:45,381
and he gave her his card.

2737
03:39:45,950 --> 03:39:51,313
She carried both up to the corner office herself, in
the lift, holding them in front of

2738
03:39:51,333 --> 03:39:53,726
her with both hands like something hot.

2739
03:39:55,297 --> 03:40:01,588
The Director read the subpoena once. It was a demand
for records — shipping, animal care,

2740
03:40:01,609 --> 03:40:07,688
facilities, power consumption — from a federal grand
jury in the District of Delaware,

2741
03:40:07,707 --> 03:40:13,787
investigating possible violations of laws he had not
heard of before that morning, and

2742
03:40:13,806 --> 03:40:16,269
would know by heart by the evening.

2743
03:40:16,840 --> 03:40:18,265
Then he read the card.

2744
03:40:18,835 --> 03:40:25,666
Special Agent Owen Carrick. Federal Bureau of
Investigation. Philadelphia Field Office.

2745
03:40:25,686 --> 03:40:28,816
Weapons of Mass Destruction Coordinator.

2746
03:40:31,005 --> 03:40:32,302
Albert? Is it bad?

2747
03:40:33,823 --> 03:40:39,454
He looked up at her. He was smiling. It was not a
nice smile. It was the smile of a man

2748
03:40:39,474 --> 03:40:44,716
who has been handed a pistol in a room full of
people who don't know it's loaded.

2749
03:41:09,834 --> 03:41:16,442
Special Agent Owen Carrick was, at forty-two, exactly
the kind of man the Federal Bureau

2750
03:41:16,462 --> 03:41:23,146
of Investigation was designed to produce: competent,
careful, and one promotion away from

2751
03:41:23,166 --> 03:41:23,976
Washington.

2752
03:41:24,545 --> 03:41:30,628
He had joined the Bureau at twenty-nine, after four
years as a prosecutor in Harrisburg.

2753
03:41:30,647 --> 03:41:36,799
He had worked public corruption, and then
counter-terrorism, and then — because it looked

2754
03:41:36,819 --> 03:41:42,693
good on a file, and because nobody else in the
field office wanted it — he had become

2755
03:41:42,713 --> 03:41:48,587
the Weapons of Mass Destruction coordinator for
eastern Pennsylvania and Delaware. It

2756
03:41:48,608 --> 03:41:55,175
was a job that mostly involved hazmat drills, and
lectures to university chemistry departments,

2757
03:41:55,195 --> 03:42:01,347
and anthrax hoaxes, and one man in Scranton who kept
trying to buy ricin on the internet.

2758
03:42:01,916 --> 03:42:07,496
He lived in Media, Pennsylvania, with his wife
Laura, who taught eighth-grade science,

2759
03:42:07,516 --> 03:42:13,485
and two daughters, seventeen and eight. He had applied
that summer for a unit chief position

2760
03:42:13,505 --> 03:42:19,150
at the Weapons of Mass Destruction Directorate at
headquarters, and the interview board

2761
03:42:19,171 --> 03:42:25,075
was in November, and he thought he had a good chance,
and he had not told anyone but Laura.

2762
03:42:25,646 --> 03:42:32,370
He had not asked for the Umbrella case. It had come
to him the way cases come to men in

2763
03:42:32,390 --> 03:42:39,425
his position — as a referral from another agency,
forwarded by a supervisor with a one-line

2764
03:42:39,445 --> 03:42:45,551
note. CDC plus FDA. Possible select agent issue.
Probably nothing. Take a look.

2765
03:42:47,121 --> 03:42:53,300
Probably nothing. That was what Owen Carrick believed,
on the fourteenth of August, twenty

2766
03:42:53,320 --> 03:42:59,637
twenty-nine, when he drove to Wilmington to serve a
grand jury subpoena on a biotech company

2767
03:42:59,657 --> 03:43:03,631
whose chief executive had been on the
cover of a magazine.

2768
03:43:04,202 --> 03:43:10,202
It was the last time in his life he would believe
anything about Albert Wesker so comfortably.

2769
03:43:12,271 --> 03:43:18,012
Doctor Samira Haddad flew up from Atlanta in
September for the joint site visit.

2770
03:43:18,583 --> 03:43:23,770
They met in the arrivals hall, and disliked each
other on sight, in the way that people

2771
03:43:23,789 --> 03:43:29,038
from different professions often do. She thought he
looked like a man who measured cases

2772
03:43:29,057 --> 03:43:34,424
by what they would do for his career. He thought she
looked like a scientist who had never

2773
03:43:34,444 --> 03:43:36,759
had to put anything in front of a jury.

2774
03:43:37,328 --> 03:43:42,628
They were, it would turn out, both right. And they
would, over the next seventeen years,

2775
03:43:42,648 --> 03:43:46,012
become the closest thing either of
them had to a friend.

2776
03:43:47,503 --> 03:43:48,824
You've read the complaint?

2777
03:43:49,344 --> 03:43:54,698
I've read it. I've read the photographs. I've read
a table of monkeys and a note in a

2778
03:43:54,718 --> 03:44:00,450
margin. I've also read that this company's biggest
product is a drug that has three hundred

2779
03:44:00,469 --> 03:44:05,823
senior citizens riding motorcycles, and that its
CEO is personally friends with — I'm

2780
03:44:05,843 --> 03:44:11,068
sorry, is personally photographed with — half the
senators on the Health Committee.

2781
03:44:11,590 --> 03:44:12,777
That's not an answer.

2782
03:44:13,298 --> 03:44:16,763
It's the context. I have to know
what I'm walking into.

2783
03:44:17,284 --> 03:44:22,511
You're walking into a building where somebody wrote
Select accordingly next to nineteen

2784
03:44:22,530 --> 03:44:23,354
dead primates.

2785
03:44:24,875 --> 03:44:30,421
Carrick did not answer. He looked at the road. And
somewhere at the back of his mind —

2786
03:44:30,441 --> 03:44:36,245
the part that had once been a prosecutor, before it
had become a careerist — a small light

2787
03:44:36,265 --> 03:44:36,763
came on.

2788
03:44:38,834 --> 03:44:44,731
The records had arrived two weeks before. Forty-one
thousand pages, in forty-three boxes,

2789
03:44:44,752 --> 03:44:50,450
delivered to the field office by a courier van on
the exact day the subpoena required,

2790
03:44:50,469 --> 03:44:56,501
indexed, cross-referenced, and bound. Shipping logs.
Animal care records. Facility permits.

2791
03:44:56,521 --> 03:45:02,219
Power bills. Catherine Adeyemi's team had produced
everything that had been asked for,

2792
03:45:02,239 --> 03:45:06,609
and a great deal that had not, and they
had produced it perfectly.

2793
03:45:07,179 --> 03:45:13,039
It was, Carrick's supervisor said, the most
cooperative subpoena response he had seen

2794
03:45:13,059 --> 03:45:14,147
in thirty years.

2795
03:45:14,717 --> 03:45:20,718
It was also, Carrick thought — looking at the
forty-three boxes stacked against his cubicle

2796
03:45:20,738 --> 03:45:26,409
wall — exactly what a company would do if it wanted
to bury an investigation in paper.

2797
03:45:28,478 --> 03:45:31,050
The Director met them at the door himself.

2798
03:45:31,620 --> 03:45:37,002
He had not been asked to. The visit had been
arranged through lawyers, as such visits

2799
03:45:37,021 --> 03:45:42,404
always are. A corporate counsel would escort them,
and a compliance officer, and they

2800
03:45:42,423 --> 03:45:47,869
would be shown the relevant facilities, and they
would be given coffee in a conference

2801
03:45:47,888 --> 03:45:49,647
room. That was the protocol.

2802
03:45:50,218 --> 03:45:56,407
Instead, at nine o'clock exactly, as the government
sedan pulled into the visitors' bay,

2803
03:45:56,427 --> 03:46:02,547
Albert Wesker walked out through the glass doors of
the tower, alone, and opened Samira

2804
03:46:02,566 --> 03:46:04,381
Haddad's car door for her.

2805
03:46:05,070 --> 03:46:10,837
Doctor Haddad. Welcome to Delaware. I read your
paper on the Nipah cluster in Kerala.

2806
03:46:10,858 --> 03:46:17,031
The contact-tracing model in section four was elegant.
Agent Carrick — thank you for coming

2807
03:46:17,051 --> 03:46:20,979
yourself. I know how many hazmat
drills you had to cancel.

2808
03:46:22,300 --> 03:46:28,012
Carrick shook the hand he was offered. It was cool
and dry and very strong, and it held

2809
03:46:28,031 --> 03:46:31,966
his a fraction of a second longer than
a handshake needs to.

2810
03:46:32,534 --> 03:46:38,639
He had been ready for lawyers. He had been ready for
obstruction, for delay, for a conference

2811
03:46:38,659 --> 03:46:44,237
room with bad coffee. He was not ready for this —
the chief executive himself, at the

2812
03:46:44,257 --> 03:46:48,452
kerb, charming, unhurried, and apparently
delighted to see them.

2813
03:46:49,021 --> 03:46:54,907
That was the point. Hospitality is a weapon. It is
the only weapon that the person you

2814
03:46:54,927 --> 03:46:56,899
use it on will thank you for.

2815
03:46:58,969 --> 03:47:00,540
He gave them everything.

2816
03:47:01,111 --> 03:47:06,582
He gave them six hours, and every floor of the tower,
and every door they asked to open,

2817
03:47:06,601 --> 03:47:12,074
and several they had not thought to ask about. He
took them through the assay programme,

2818
03:47:12,093 --> 03:47:17,691
and the Aeterna trial ward, where Arthur Kowalczyk —
who happened to be in for his monthly

2819
03:47:17,710 --> 03:47:23,119
check-up that morning — told Samira Haddad at some
length about his motorcycle. He took

2820
03:47:23,139 --> 03:47:28,673
them to the loading dock, and introduced them to
Deshawn. He showed them the power bills.

2821
03:47:29,244 --> 03:47:35,075
And then, when Haddad raised the photograph — the
grey figure in the capsule — he took

2822
03:47:35,094 --> 03:47:40,993
them to the lobby of the Singapore annex on the
tower's ground floor, where, in a glass

2823
03:47:41,012 --> 03:47:46,843
case, beneath a spotlight, stood a nine-foot
sculpture of a grey, hairless figure in a

2824
03:47:46,862 --> 03:47:50,515
frosted cylinder, with a small brass
plaque beside it.

2825
03:47:51,086 --> 03:47:57,711
The Sentinel. A commission by a young sculptor from
Rhode Island, for Umbrella's campaign

2826
03:47:57,731 --> 03:48:02,863
on biosecurity. Resin, steel, and acrylic.
Two thousand twenty-eight.

2827
03:48:04,554 --> 03:48:06,880
You're telling me that's what's in
the photograph.

2828
03:48:07,399 --> 03:48:13,536
I'm telling you that's what Doctor Okafor
photographed. Doctor Marsh's team used a picture

2829
03:48:13,556 --> 03:48:20,101
of it on a slide about public perception. The label on
the page is a working title. T-zero-zero.

2830
03:48:20,120 --> 03:48:24,136
The zero-zero is because the sculptor
hadn't signed it yet.

2831
03:48:25,255 --> 03:48:31,289
Pell had commissioned the sculpture in April, from a
real artist, who had been paid real

2832
03:48:31,308 --> 03:48:37,341
money and would go on to have a real career. It had
taken her ten weeks. The invoice was

2833
03:48:37,361 --> 03:48:38,716
in box twenty-seven.

2834
03:48:40,787 --> 03:48:42,540
The macaques were Evelyn's.

2835
03:48:43,111 --> 03:48:49,289
She met them in a conference room on the ninth floor,
in a crisp white coat, with a folder,

2836
03:48:49,308 --> 03:48:55,213
and she explained — clearly, patiently, in the tone
of a scientist explaining something

2837
03:48:55,234 --> 03:49:01,343
slightly embarrassing to a colleague — that the
twenty-four rhesus macaques from Singapore

2838
03:49:01,363 --> 03:49:07,134
had been part of a standard toxicology study for
Aeterna, conducted under contract by

2839
03:49:07,154 --> 03:49:13,332
a licensed research organisation in Maryland. The
contract was in box fifteen. The Maryland

2840
03:49:13,351 --> 03:49:16,670
facility's inspection reports
were in box twelve.

2841
03:49:17,359 --> 03:49:22,970
In a toxicology study the animals are humanely
euthanised at the end of the dosing period

2842
03:49:22,990 --> 03:49:28,539
so that their tissues can be examined. That's what
deceased means in the table. Nineteen

2843
03:49:28,558 --> 03:49:34,043
at the scheduled end-point. The five marked enhanced
were an enhanced-monitoring cohort

2844
03:49:34,064 --> 03:49:39,485
— animals kept for a longer observation window.
They're still alive. You're welcome to

2845
03:49:39,504 --> 03:49:40,182
visit them.

2846
03:49:40,700 --> 03:49:43,524
And the note in the margin?
Select accordingly?

2847
03:49:44,043 --> 03:49:49,596
Group housing. Macaques are social, and it's better
for their welfare if we house them

2848
03:49:49,617 --> 03:49:55,168
together, but animals with high baseline aggression
don't tolerate co-housing well. So

2849
03:49:55,189 --> 03:50:01,129
we select the calmer ones for the group enclosures.
It's in our animal welfare plan. Section

2850
03:50:01,149 --> 03:50:01,389
six.

2851
03:50:03,110 --> 03:50:09,604
It was a good answer. It was, in fact, word for
word, a real section of a real animal

2852
03:50:09,625 --> 03:50:16,351
welfare plan, which Pell had written and backdated in
March, the week after Nadia Okafor

2853
03:50:16,370 --> 03:50:17,039
resigned.

2854
03:50:17,611 --> 03:50:23,570
Samira Haddad wrote it down. She had been an epidemic
intelligence officer for four years,

2855
03:50:23,591 --> 03:50:29,286
and she knew what she was looking at: an answer to
every question, before the question

2856
03:50:29,306 --> 03:50:35,134
had been asked. It did not mean they were lying. It
meant they had known she was coming.

2857
03:50:35,703 --> 03:50:37,313
Which, of course, they had.

2858
03:50:39,380 --> 03:50:45,302
The last stop was the old building. Haddad had
insisted on it. The complaint had mentioned

2859
03:50:45,323 --> 03:50:51,309
a sublevel, and the power bills had been
extraordinary, and she wanted to see the basement.

2860
03:50:51,880 --> 03:50:57,402
So they went down in the freight elevator — the
Director, the two investigators, and a

2861
03:50:57,422 --> 03:51:02,943
tall man in a black suit who had joined them without
introduction in the lobby and who

2862
03:51:02,963 --> 03:51:07,712
stood in the corner of the elevator with his
hands folded in front of him.

2863
03:51:08,282 --> 03:51:14,038
The elevator panel had two buttons. Loading dock.
Basement. Carrick looked at it for a

2864
03:51:14,057 --> 03:51:19,879
long moment. There was no third button. There was a
smooth, blank steel plate below the

2865
03:51:19,899 --> 03:51:25,789
basement button, with four screws in it, and a very
faint rectangular mark in the paint,

2866
03:51:25,809 --> 03:51:28,274
as though something had been removed.

2867
03:51:28,843 --> 03:51:34,801
The basement had the twenty old freezers, and six
new ones, and the vault that Pell had

2868
03:51:34,822 --> 03:51:40,986
bought for eighteen hundred Points to show Catherine
eighteen months before. It was clean,

2869
03:51:41,005 --> 03:51:47,307
and cold, and it used, the Director explained, a great
deal of power, because it was holding

2870
03:51:47,326 --> 03:51:53,216
thirty years of biological samples at minus one
hundred and fifty degrees, and because

2871
03:51:53,236 --> 03:51:57,475
the old building's electrical system
was, frankly, a disgrace.

2872
03:51:58,043 --> 03:52:02,888
The corner where the grey machine had stood was
bare concrete, freshly painted.

2873
03:52:04,779 --> 03:52:05,932
What was here before?

2874
03:52:06,451 --> 03:52:12,615
An air handling unit. Arcturus. We replaced it last
year. The state inspected it in twenty

2875
03:52:12,636 --> 03:52:17,495
twenty-six, if you'd like the report. Mister
Delgado was very thorough.

2876
03:52:19,014 --> 03:52:24,816
He had said the name of the inspector. Carrick
wrote it down. He did not know why. He

2877
03:52:24,836 --> 03:52:30,637
would find the report, four months later, in a
state database, and read it, and begin

2878
03:52:30,657 --> 03:52:36,528
to wonder why a small research lab in twenty
twenty-six had gone so far out of its way

2879
03:52:36,548 --> 03:52:37,622
to be inspected.

2880
03:52:39,692 --> 03:52:44,760
It was at the very end of the day, in the lobby,
that Owen Carrick noticed the thing he

2881
03:52:44,780 --> 03:52:47,509
would think about for the next
seventeen years.

2882
03:52:48,077 --> 03:52:53,825
He was waiting for Haddad, who had gone to the
restroom. The Director had excused himself

2883
03:52:53,844 --> 03:52:59,461
to take a call. And the tall man in the black suit —
the one who had ridden down in the

2884
03:52:59,481 --> 03:53:05,292
elevator with them without being introduced — was
standing by the lifts, twenty feet away,

2885
03:53:05,313 --> 03:53:09,049
with his hands folded in front of him,
looking at nothing.

2886
03:53:09,620 --> 03:53:15,147
Carrick watched him. He did not know why. It was a
cop's habit, from long before he had

2887
03:53:15,167 --> 03:53:20,184
become a careerist — you watch the person in the
room who isn't doing anything.

2888
03:53:20,754 --> 03:53:23,997
He watched him for a minute. Then
two. Then three.

2889
03:53:26,067 --> 03:53:29,673
In three minutes, the man in the black
suit did not blink.

2890
03:53:30,243 --> 03:53:36,069
Carrick knew it was not possible. A human being
blinks between fifteen and twenty times

2891
03:53:36,090 --> 03:53:41,782
a minute. You can stop yourself for a while —
thirty seconds, perhaps a minute if you

2892
03:53:41,802 --> 03:53:47,629
concentrate — but your eyes begin to water and burn.
This man's eyes were not watering.

2893
03:53:47,648 --> 03:53:53,476
They were perfectly clear and perfectly still, and
he was not concentrating on anything

2894
03:53:53,495 --> 03:53:53,945
at all.

2895
03:53:54,514 --> 03:54:00,140
At three minutes and forty seconds, as if he had felt
himself being counted, the man turned

2896
03:54:00,161 --> 03:54:03,677
his head and looked directly at
Carrick across the lobby.

2897
03:54:04,246 --> 03:54:07,181
Then he blinked. Once. Slowly.
Like a courtesy.

2898
03:54:09,251 --> 03:54:14,762
The Director walked them to their car. He opened the
door for Haddad again. He thanked

2899
03:54:14,782 --> 03:54:20,295
them both, sincerely, for their time and their
diligence, and said that Umbrella would

2900
03:54:20,315 --> 03:54:25,762
cooperate with anything else the government needed,
and that he hoped they would come

2901
03:54:25,782 --> 03:54:26,083
back.

2902
03:54:26,655 --> 03:54:32,427
Then, as Carrick walked around to the driver's side,
the Director said, quite pleasantly,

2903
03:54:32,448 --> 03:54:34,706
in a voice pitched for no one else:

2904
03:54:35,396 --> 03:54:41,304
Good luck with the unit chief board in November, Agent
Carrick. I hear it's a strong field.

2905
03:54:43,323 --> 03:54:45,933
Carrick stopped with his hand on
the car door.

2906
03:54:46,502 --> 03:54:52,476
He had told no one. Not his supervisor. Not his
partner. Not his friends. He had told

2907
03:54:52,495 --> 03:54:57,692
his wife, at their kitchen table, one night in
June, with the window open.

2908
03:54:58,263 --> 03:55:04,070
He looked up. The Director was already walking back
toward the glass doors, unhurried,

2909
03:55:04,091 --> 03:55:07,798
hands in the pockets of his coat. He
did not look back.

2910
03:55:10,617 --> 03:55:14,608
Assessment. Federal inspection
neutralised.

2911
03:55:17,948 --> 03:55:21,836
They drove north on the interstate in
silence for twenty minutes.

2912
03:55:22,525 --> 03:55:25,800
Well. That was the cleanest facility I
have ever inspected.

2913
03:55:26,322 --> 03:55:26,985
Yeah.

2914
03:55:27,504 --> 03:55:33,101
Every answer ready. Every document in a numbered
box. A sculpture, for God's sake. Owen

2915
03:55:33,120 --> 03:55:35,554
— they knew we were coming for months.

2916
03:55:36,074 --> 03:55:41,219
They knew we were coming for months because we sent
them a subpoena, Samira. That's not

2917
03:55:41,239 --> 03:55:41,694
a crime.

2918
03:55:43,216 --> 03:55:44,342
So you're closing it.

2919
03:55:46,361 --> 03:55:52,004
He should have. Every instinct that had got him to
where he was — every instinct that

2920
03:55:52,024 --> 03:55:58,334
was about to get him to Washington — told him to close
it. A cooperative company. A disgruntled

2921
03:55:58,354 --> 03:56:04,265
former employee. A clean inspection. A sculpture in a
glass case. A file that would look,

2922
03:56:04,286 --> 03:56:09,995
to an interview board in November, like a careful
agent doing careful work and knowing

2923
03:56:10,015 --> 03:56:10,862
when to stop.

2924
03:56:11,554 --> 03:56:12,759
He didn't blink.

2925
03:56:13,279 --> 03:56:13,833
What?

2926
03:56:14,351 --> 03:56:19,816
The guard. The big one, by the lifts. I watched him
for almost four minutes. He didn't

2927
03:56:19,836 --> 03:56:20,519
blink once.

2928
03:56:22,039 --> 03:56:23,554
That's — people can do that.

2929
03:56:24,074 --> 03:56:25,260
No. They can't.

2930
03:56:27,280 --> 03:56:33,027
He drove on in silence. The rain began, and he
turned on the wipers, and watched them

2931
03:56:33,047 --> 03:56:33,842
for a while.

2932
03:56:34,411 --> 03:56:40,565
He did not close the file. He did not decide not to
close it, either. He simply did not

2933
03:56:40,584 --> 03:56:46,880
close it that night, or the next, and the file sat in
the tray on his desk, under a stack

2934
03:56:46,900 --> 03:56:49,718
of hazmat drill reports, for eight days.

2935
03:56:50,289 --> 03:56:56,470
On the twelfth day, a man called the Philadelphia field
office from a pay phone at a highway

2936
03:57:15,936 --> 03:57:19,550
You were in the building. On the
eleventh. I saw your car.

2937
03:57:20,070 --> 03:57:21,060
Who is this?

2938
03:57:21,578 --> 03:57:27,537
You watched the man by the lifts. The big one. For about
four minutes. I was on the mezzanine.

2939
03:57:27,557 --> 03:57:28,746
I saw you counting.

2940
03:57:30,466 --> 03:57:32,458
Owen Carrick sat up in his chair.

2941
03:57:33,150 --> 03:57:38,554
He doesn't blink, does he? None of them do. The new
ones. I've been counting for three

2942
03:57:38,574 --> 03:57:38,933
years.

2943
03:57:39,452 --> 03:57:40,750
I'd like to meet you.

2944
03:57:41,271 --> 03:57:43,854
I know you would. That's why I called.

2945
03:57:45,873 --> 03:57:51,978
They met at dawn, in a diner forty miles from
Wilmington, because Howard Lessing did not

2946
03:57:51,998 --> 03:57:53,578
trust any place closer.

2947
03:57:54,149 --> 03:57:59,248
He had lost weight since Carrick had glimpsed him on
the mezzanine. He held his coffee

2948
03:57:59,269 --> 03:58:04,310
with both hands, the way he had held it at a staff
meeting three years before, and he

2949
03:58:04,329 --> 03:58:09,371
talked for two hours without eating, and Carrick —
who had interviewed murderers, and

2950
03:58:09,391 --> 03:58:14,909
the parents of murdered children, and a man who had
tried to buy ricin in Scranton — listened

2951
03:58:14,929 --> 03:58:19,970
to him with the growing, sick sensation of a man
who realises halfway through a story

2952
03:58:19,989 --> 03:58:21,816
that he is going to believe it.

2953
03:58:22,505 --> 03:58:27,789
It was a blood sample. His. He gave it to a
technician with a label that said Control

2954
03:58:27,809 --> 03:58:33,154
Seven and told him it was for calibration. I saw the
printout. Cells dividing at forty

2955
03:58:33,174 --> 03:58:38,707
times the normal rate and not becoming cancer. A
viral genome that doesn't match anything

2956
03:58:38,727 --> 03:58:44,198
in any database on Earth. I copied it. And he knew.
He knew the same night. He came into

2957
03:58:44,218 --> 03:58:49,875
the lab. The door didn't open, Agent Carrick. I was
watching the door. He was just — there.

2958
03:58:50,396 --> 03:58:51,326
And then?

2959
03:58:51,846 --> 03:58:53,845
And then he took off his glasses.

2960
03:58:55,864 --> 03:59:01,497
Lessing stopped. His hands had begun, very slightly,
to shake, and he put the cup down

2961
03:59:01,516 --> 03:59:03,600
and flattened them on the table.

2962
03:59:04,292 --> 03:59:10,923
His eyes are red. Not bloodshot. Red. Like a coal.
And the pupils go thin. Up and down.

2963
03:59:10,943 --> 03:59:12,069
Like a snake's.

2964
03:59:13,789 --> 03:59:19,784
Carrick wrote it down. He wrote it down exactly as it
was said, because that was procedure,

2965
03:59:19,804 --> 03:59:25,404
and because he was already thinking about how it
would read to a supervisor, and to a

2966
03:59:25,424 --> 03:59:31,088
prosecutor, and to an interview board in November.
Eyes red, pupils vertical. It would

2967
03:59:31,109 --> 03:59:35,186
read, he knew, like the statement of a
man having a breakdown.

2968
03:59:35,757 --> 03:59:41,190
And he wrote it down anyway, and underlined it,
because he had spent three minutes and

2969
03:59:41,210 --> 03:59:43,663
forty seconds watching a man not blink.

2970
03:59:45,353 --> 03:59:47,218
What do you want, Doctor Lessing?

2971
03:59:47,739 --> 03:59:53,077
I want someone to go down there. There's something
under that building. The power, the

2972
03:59:53,096 --> 03:59:58,809
liquid nitrogen, the elevator — Evelyn Marsh goes down
in that elevator at night and doesn't

2973
03:59:58,829 --> 04:00:04,354
come up for nine hours. There is something under the
basement. And there's something that

2974
04:00:04,373 --> 04:00:04,914
breathes.

2975
04:00:05,433 --> 04:00:07,028
What do you want for yourself?

2976
04:00:09,049 --> 04:00:14,609
Lessing looked at him for a long time. When he
answered, his voice had changed. It had

2977
04:00:14,629 --> 04:00:20,123
lost its tremor. It had become, for the first time,
the voice of the man who had been

2978
04:00:20,144 --> 04:00:23,822
promised a prize at a conference in
nineteen ninety-four.

2979
04:00:24,513 --> 04:00:26,604
I want to be the one who was right.

2980
04:00:28,623 --> 04:00:34,661
He opened Howard Lessing as a confidential human
source on the twenty-sixth of September.

2981
04:00:34,681 --> 04:00:40,786
The paperwork took nine days to clear. The codename,
which Carrick chose because it amused

2982
04:00:40,806 --> 04:00:45,277
him for about thirty seconds and then
never again, was Calibrator.

2983
04:00:45,847 --> 04:00:52,654
His supervisor, an assistant special agent in charge
named Janet Kowal, signed the authorisation

2984
04:00:52,674 --> 04:00:58,057
on the ninth day. Then she closed her office
door and asked him to sit down.

2985
04:00:58,747 --> 04:01:04,009
I've had two calls this week about Umbrella. One from
the U-S Attorney, who wants to know

2986
04:01:04,029 --> 04:01:09,233
why we're still looking at a company that answered a
subpoena with forty thousand pages.

2987
04:01:09,253 --> 04:01:14,278
One from a staffer on the Senate Health Committee,
who wanted to know — very politely

2988
04:01:14,298 --> 04:01:19,501
— whether the Bureau was aware that Aeterna is saving
the lives of American seniors, and

2989
04:01:19,521 --> 04:01:21,698
whether we had considered the optics.

2990
04:01:22,218 --> 04:01:23,618
I've considered them.

2991
04:01:24,139 --> 04:01:29,300
Owen. You have a board in November. You're the
strongest candidate from this office in

2992
04:01:29,319 --> 04:01:34,481
five years. Don't hang your career on a
whistleblower who says the CEO has snake eyes.

2993
04:01:36,002 --> 04:01:37,441
He didn't blink, Janet.

2994
04:01:37,961 --> 04:01:38,593
What?

2995
04:01:39,112 --> 04:01:40,592
Nothing. I'll be careful.

2996
04:01:42,612 --> 04:01:47,264
The law came next, in its own careful time,
and the law was Umbrella's.

2997
04:01:47,834 --> 04:01:53,811
The company's outside counsel filed a motion to
quash the grand jury's second subpoena

2998
04:01:53,832 --> 04:02:00,090
on the grounds that it was overbroad, and won. They
filed a request for a protective order

2999
04:02:00,110 --> 04:02:06,018
over the Aeterna trial data on the grounds that it
was a trade secret worth billions,

3000
04:02:06,038 --> 04:02:12,226
and won that too. They produced, when the court
ordered them to produce anything, so much

3001
04:02:12,246 --> 04:02:18,643
of it, so fast, so perfectly indexed, that the United
States Attorney's office in Wilmington

3002
04:02:18,663 --> 04:02:24,921
— which had four lawyers assigned to the whole of its
white-collar docket — began to refer

3003
04:02:24,941 --> 04:02:27,781
to the Umbrella boxes simply as the wall.

3004
04:02:28,350 --> 04:02:33,824
Carrick had the wall in his office. By November it
reached the ceiling on two sides.

3005
04:02:34,394 --> 04:02:40,011
He did not miss the interview board. He went, in
his best suit, to Washington, and he

3006
04:02:40,031 --> 04:02:46,179
answered every question well, and when they asked him
about his current caseload he mentioned

3007
04:02:46,199 --> 04:02:51,086
hazmat drills, and a man in Scranton, and did
not mention Umbrella at all.

3008
04:02:51,656 --> 04:02:57,391
He did not get the job. It went to an agent from
Chicago. He would never know — because

3009
04:02:57,412 --> 04:03:03,280
nobody ever told him, and because it did not happen
in any way that could be written down

3010
04:03:03,300 --> 04:03:09,167
— that one of the three people on the board had
received, the week before, an unsolicited

3011
04:03:09,186 --> 04:03:14,790
and very generous offer to join a biosecurity
advisory council funded by the Umbrella

3012
04:03:14,810 --> 04:03:20,479
Foundation, and had been thinking, during Owen
Carrick's interview, about the stipend.

3013
04:03:22,548 --> 04:03:24,612
The Director knew the day it happened.

3014
04:03:25,182 --> 04:03:30,972
He had known Howard Lessing's face for three years
now. He knew its guilt, and its fear,

3015
04:03:30,993 --> 04:03:36,586
and the long slow decay of that fear, like the
half-life of an isotope, and the spite

3016
04:03:36,605 --> 04:03:42,331
that had begun to grow back in the space it left. He
had watched it every Monday across

3017
04:03:42,351 --> 04:03:44,576
a table, and filed it, and waited.

3018
04:03:45,146 --> 04:03:50,381
On Monday the first of October, Howard Lessing came
into the leadership meeting with a

3019
04:03:50,400 --> 04:03:51,298
different face.

3020
04:03:51,867 --> 04:03:57,668
Not braver. Lessing was not a brave man. It was
something the Cold-Read took a moment

3021
04:03:57,689 --> 04:04:03,489
to name, because it was rare, and because he had
not expected to see it in Lessing at

3022
04:04:03,510 --> 04:04:03,764
all.

3023
04:04:04,334 --> 04:04:05,564
It was purpose.

3024
04:04:07,452 --> 04:04:09,096
He has gone to them.

3025
04:04:09,617 --> 04:04:10,257
Yes.

3026
04:04:10,778 --> 04:04:15,692
He is an informant. For the police. In
your house. At your table.

3027
04:04:16,211 --> 04:04:20,906
For the Federal Bureau of Investigation. Try
to keep up with the century.

3028
04:04:21,427 --> 04:04:27,735
Then end it. Tonight. You have a creature in the
ground that no one knows exists. You

3029
04:04:27,755 --> 04:04:32,948
have six men who do not show up on a camera.
You have a thousand ways.

3030
04:04:34,468 --> 04:04:35,087
No.

3031
04:04:35,608 --> 04:04:41,597
I told you. Three years ago, in the car. I told you
that you had made a man who hated

3032
04:04:41,618 --> 04:04:45,487
you. I told you hate shows up to
everything eventually.

3033
04:04:46,007 --> 04:04:48,418
And it's shown up. Good. Let him
wear the wire.

3034
04:04:50,439 --> 04:04:55,949
It was the purest distillation of the Wesker half
of the mind that the Adolf half had

3035
04:04:55,969 --> 04:05:01,219
yet seen, and it disturbed the old voice more
than anything had since the bunker.

3036
04:05:01,788 --> 04:05:07,686
A traitor, discovered, is a problem. A traitor,
discovered, who does not know he has been

3037
04:05:07,707 --> 04:05:13,340
discovered, is a radio station. He broadcasts to
his handlers whatever he believes to

3038
04:05:13,360 --> 04:05:18,194
be true. And if you control what he believes,
you control what they hear.

3039
04:05:18,884 --> 04:05:23,957
Every Monday, Howard will hear something. A little
piece of the company. A budget line.

3040
04:05:23,977 --> 04:05:29,226
A shipment. A rumour about a new facility in Nevada.
And every Monday night, he will drive

3041
04:05:29,246 --> 04:05:34,377
to a pay phone and tell Owen Carrick. And Owen
Carrick will write it down, and chase it,

3042
04:05:34,397 --> 04:05:38,300
and find — each time — exactly what I've
put there for him to find.

3043
04:05:38,819 --> 04:05:41,040
And when he finds nothing?

3044
04:05:41,560 --> 04:05:46,939
Then he'll start to doubt his source. And then his
source will start to doubt himself.

3045
04:05:46,959 --> 04:05:52,400
And then, one day, Howard Lessing will make a
mistake that I haven't arranged. And that

3046
04:05:52,421 --> 04:05:54,472
one — that one I'll be ready for.

3047
04:05:57,122 --> 04:06:02,670
Assessment. Hostile source
identified. Channel acquired.

3048
04:06:04,487 --> 04:06:09,055
The System agreed with him. It usually did,
when he was being clever.

3049
04:06:09,623 --> 04:06:13,833
It would not agree with him about Howard
Lessing for very much longer.

3050
04:06:15,902 --> 04:06:19,498
For three months, it worked exactly as
he had said it would.

3051
04:06:20,069 --> 04:06:25,968
On Mondays, Howard Lessing overheard things. A
remark from Catherine about a new data

3052
04:06:25,988 --> 04:06:32,235
centre in Nevada, which was real, and which held
nothing but servers. A shipment of liquid

3053
04:06:32,255 --> 04:06:38,432
nitrogen that turned out, when Carrick traced it, to
be for the Basel vault. A late-night

3054
04:06:38,452 --> 04:06:44,559
conversation between two of the research cohort about
a primate study, which led Carrick

3055
04:06:44,579 --> 04:06:50,895
to a licensed facility in Maryland, where he found
forty perfectly healthy rhesus macaques,

3056
04:06:50,915 --> 04:06:55,143
and an inspector who was very annoyed
to be asked about them.

3057
04:06:55,712 --> 04:07:01,868
Every lead was real. Every lead went nowhere. And every
Monday night, on a pay phone somewhere

3058
04:07:01,888 --> 04:07:07,780
different, Howard Lessing sounded a little less
certain, and Owen Carrick sounded a little

3059
04:07:07,800 --> 04:07:08,502
more tired.

3060
04:07:09,073 --> 04:07:14,963
Lessing tried, once, in November, to photograph the
freight elevator panel at night. He

3061
04:07:14,984 --> 04:07:21,012
waited until the building was empty. He went down to
the basement with his phone. He took

3062
04:07:21,032 --> 04:07:24,274
nineteen pictures of the panel
from every angle.

3063
04:07:24,843 --> 04:07:30,820
There were two buttons in every picture. Loading
dock. Basement. And a smooth steel plate

3064
04:07:30,841 --> 04:07:32,911
beneath them, with four screws.

3065
04:07:33,480 --> 04:07:39,363
He had seen Evelyn Marsh go down past that plate with
his own eyes. He knew it was there.

3066
04:07:39,382 --> 04:07:45,066
And he stood in the empty freight elevator at
midnight, staring at the pictures on his

3067
04:07:45,086 --> 04:07:50,836
phone, and for the first time in three years, Howard
Lessing began to wonder whether he

3068
04:07:50,855 --> 04:07:51,765
was going mad.

3069
04:07:53,834 --> 04:07:59,552
The call that changed everything came a week before
Christmas, to his own phone, at his

3070
04:07:59,573 --> 04:08:00,739
own kitchen table.

3071
04:08:01,310 --> 04:08:06,877
It was not from Carrick. It was not from Umbrella.
It was from a number in New Jersey,

3072
04:08:06,897 --> 04:08:12,398
and when Lessing answered it, a smooth, familiar
voice that he had not heard in three

3073
04:08:12,418 --> 04:08:13,113
years said:

3074
04:08:13,802 --> 04:08:16,918
Howard. It's Simon Ferris. You
probably don't remember me.

3075
04:08:17,436 --> 04:08:18,547
I remember you.

3076
04:08:19,068 --> 04:08:23,889
I'm at Halvorsen Pike now. Head of external innovation. I
was going through some old correspondence

3077
04:08:23,909 --> 04:08:28,192
the other day, and I found an email you wrote me in
the autumn of twenty twenty-six. You

3078
04:08:28,212 --> 04:08:32,398
never sent it, I know — but it was in the drafts on
a server we took over when Verdant

3079
04:08:32,418 --> 04:08:35,773
went down. Something about data of
extraordinary commercial interest.

3080
04:08:37,792 --> 04:08:39,658
Lessing did not say anything.

3081
04:08:40,228 --> 04:08:46,079
He was thinking — with the slow, dreadful clarity of a
man who has run out of other options

3082
04:08:46,099 --> 04:08:51,950
— about the Federal Bureau of Investigation, which had
spent three months chasing shipments

3083
04:08:51,970 --> 04:08:57,693
of liquid nitrogen. About a freight elevator with a
button that did not photograph. About

3084
04:08:57,712 --> 04:09:03,435
a man in a lab in the middle of the night whose eyes
had turned red. About a machine that

3085
04:09:03,454 --> 04:09:04,016
breathed.

3086
04:09:04,585 --> 04:09:11,111
And about the fact that Halvorsen Pike, the second
largest pharmaceutical company on Earth,

3087
04:09:11,131 --> 04:09:17,657
was losing eighteen billion dollars a year to Aeterna,
and would do — he suspected — almost

3088
04:09:17,676 --> 04:09:20,318
anything to find out how it was made.

3089
04:09:21,010 --> 04:09:22,551
Howard? Are you still there?

3090
04:09:24,569 --> 04:09:27,281
I'm here, Simon. I think we should talk.

3091
04:09:29,301 --> 04:09:34,588
He did not tell Owen Carrick about the call. It was
the first thing in three months he

3092
04:09:34,609 --> 04:09:35,639
had not told him.

3093
04:09:54,505 --> 04:09:57,125
There were four of them, and they
were very good.

3094
04:09:57,694 --> 04:10:03,380
They had been soldiers once — two of them in the
same special operations unit, two in

3095
04:10:03,400 --> 04:10:09,087
the British Army — and then they had become the
kind of men that large companies hire

3096
04:10:09,108 --> 04:10:15,063
when they need something done that nobody will ever
write down. They worked for a private

3097
04:10:15,083 --> 04:10:20,836
security firm in Virginia that had been retained,
through three layers of consultants,

3098
04:10:20,856 --> 04:10:26,543
by the competitive intelligence department of
Halvorsen Pike. They had been told that

3099
04:10:26,564 --> 04:10:32,451
the job was simple: enter a research facility in
Wilmington with the help of an insider,

3100
04:10:32,471 --> 04:10:38,695
copy the contents of a single air-gapped server,
photograph anything unusual in the basement,

3101
04:10:38,716 --> 04:10:39,367
and leave.

3102
04:10:39,936 --> 04:10:45,083
They had been told it would take twelve minutes. They
had been told nobody would be hurt.

3103
04:10:45,654 --> 04:10:50,347
Both of these things were believed, sincerely,
by the people who had told them.

3104
04:10:52,417 --> 04:10:57,792
Howard Lessing let them in at twelve fifty-two,
through the loading dock door, with his

3105
04:10:57,813 --> 04:10:58,414
own badge.

3106
04:10:58,984 --> 04:11:04,390
He had spent three weeks telling himself why. He
had told himself that the Bureau was

3107
04:11:04,411 --> 04:11:09,817
too slow, that the Bureau had been captured, that
the Bureau would chase shipments of

3108
04:11:09,837 --> 04:11:15,246
liquid nitrogen until everybody was dead. He had
told himself that four professionals

3109
04:11:15,265 --> 04:11:20,927
with thermal cameras and ground-penetrating equipment
would see what his phone could not.

3110
04:11:20,948 --> 04:11:26,420
That they would come out with photographs of
whatever was under the basement, and that

3111
04:11:26,440 --> 04:11:31,975
he would take those photographs to Owen Carrick, and
to Hannah Price, and to the world.

3112
04:11:31,995 --> 04:11:35,549
That he would finally, finally be the
one who was right.

3113
04:11:36,121 --> 04:11:41,854
He had not told himself what Halvorsen Pike was
paying him. He did not need to. It was

3114
04:11:41,875 --> 04:11:47,743
a great deal of money, and it was already in an
account in Cyprus, and he was trying not

3115
04:11:47,763 --> 04:11:48,948
to think about it.

3116
04:11:51,016 --> 04:11:53,234
They went down to the basement first.

3117
04:11:53,805 --> 04:11:59,904
It was dark, and cold, and it smelled of refrigerant
and old concrete. The four men moved

3118
04:11:59,924 --> 04:12:05,885
through it the way they had been trained to move
through houses in other countries — in

3119
04:12:05,905 --> 04:12:11,798
a line, weapons low, each covering a sector. Their
thermal cameras swept the freezers,

3120
04:12:11,817 --> 04:12:17,228
the new vault, the bare concrete corner where a
grey machine had once breathed.

3121
04:12:17,918 --> 04:12:23,156
Contact. There's heat under the floor. A lot of
heat. Big volume. Thirty, forty metres

3122
04:12:23,175 --> 04:12:23,461
down.

3123
04:12:23,862 --> 04:12:26,391
That's not a basement. That's a building.

3124
04:12:27,912 --> 04:12:33,185
Lessing, standing behind them by the freight
elevator, felt something in his chest go

3125
04:12:33,206 --> 04:12:37,420
very light. He had been right. He had been
right. They could see it.

3126
04:12:38,989 --> 04:12:42,567
And then, behind him, the freight
elevator panel lit up.

3127
04:12:43,137 --> 04:12:48,710
He turned. He saw it happen — the thing he had tried
for three months to photograph, the

3128
04:12:48,730 --> 04:12:54,305
thing he had begun to believe he had imagined. Below
the button for the basement, in the

3129
04:12:54,325 --> 04:12:59,835
smooth steel plate with the four screws, a third
button was glowing. Black, with a thin

3130
04:12:59,854 --> 04:13:05,746
crimson ring. And the floor indicator above the doors,
which had only ever shown two numbers,

3131
04:13:05,765 --> 04:13:09,305
was showing a third one, and the
third one was climbing.

3132
04:13:09,875 --> 04:13:11,380
Something was coming up.

3133
04:13:13,272 --> 04:13:15,747
Oh, God. Oh, God, he's here.

3134
04:13:17,067 --> 04:13:19,118
What? Who's here? Doc — who's here?

3135
04:13:19,638 --> 04:13:25,318
Lessing did not answer. He was backing away from the
elevator. He was making a sound —

3136
04:13:25,338 --> 04:13:31,020
a thin, high sound, like a kettle. One of the
soldiers, a young man from Tennessee who

3137
04:13:31,040 --> 04:13:36,786
had been awake for thirty hours and had been told
nobody would be here, swung his rifle

3138
04:13:36,806 --> 04:13:42,620
toward the noise, and toward the glowing panel, and
toward the old man who was suddenly,

3139
04:13:42,639 --> 04:13:44,210
inexplicably, screaming.

3140
04:13:46,281 --> 04:13:51,873
He fired three times. He would say afterwards — to
the one person he ever told — that

3141
04:13:51,894 --> 04:13:58,014
he had not meant to. That the old man had moved. That
he had thought the old man was reaching

3142
04:13:58,034 --> 04:13:58,940
for something.

3143
04:13:59,507 --> 04:14:04,951
Howard Lessing fell against the freezers, and slid
down them, and sat on the cold floor

3144
04:14:04,971 --> 04:14:10,415
of the basement where he had spent thirty-four years
of his working life, with his hand

3145
04:14:10,434 --> 04:14:11,733
pressed to his chest.

3146
04:14:12,304 --> 04:14:16,951
He was looking at the elevator. At the black
button. At the number climbing.

3147
04:14:17,520 --> 04:14:23,202
He had been right. He had wanted, more than anything
in his life, to be the one who was

3148
04:14:23,222 --> 04:14:29,363
right, and he was. It was the last thing he knew, and —
the Cold-Read would tell the Director,

3149
04:14:29,384 --> 04:14:35,328
thirty seconds later, reading what was left in the old
man's face — he died, of all things,

3150
04:14:35,349 --> 04:14:35,985
satisfied.

3151
04:14:38,554 --> 04:14:40,362
The elevator doors opened.

3152
04:14:40,932 --> 04:14:46,389
He had been in Arklay. He had been working late, as
he did every Sunday, and Hale had

3153
04:14:46,409 --> 04:14:51,867
told him eleven seconds ago that the loading dock
door had opened on Doctor Lessing's

3154
04:14:51,887 --> 04:14:57,408
badge with four men behind him. He had been in the
elevator when the shots were fired.

3155
04:14:57,429 --> 04:14:59,728
He had heard them through the shaft.

3156
04:15:00,299 --> 04:15:06,200
He stepped out. He saw Howard Lessing on the floor.
He saw four men with rifles. And he

3157
04:15:06,219 --> 04:15:11,985
felt — for the first time in either of his lives —
two completely different things at

3158
04:15:12,005 --> 04:15:13,686
exactly the same instant.

3159
04:15:14,755 --> 04:15:20,426
The Wesker half felt fury. Cold, exact,
professional fury, the fury of a chess player

3160
04:15:20,447 --> 04:15:26,654
whose opponent has knocked the board over. Because this
could not be hidden. A dead employee.

3161
04:15:26,674 --> 04:15:32,612
Four armed men. Gunshots. Nothing about this could
ever be made to disappear. Three years

3162
04:15:32,632 --> 04:15:38,438
of silence, of deniability, of controlled burns and
clean rooms and sculptures in glass

3163
04:15:38,458 --> 04:15:44,195
cases — ended, by a frightened boy from Tennessee
who had been awake for thirty hours.

3164
04:15:44,765 --> 04:15:46,738
And the Adolf half felt joy.

3165
04:15:48,426 --> 04:15:49,686
At last.

3166
04:15:50,708 --> 04:15:56,349
He used the Thought Acceleration. He had not planned
to. It simply happened, the way a

3167
04:15:56,369 --> 04:15:59,180
hand closes when something is
thrown at it.

3168
04:15:59,751 --> 04:16:05,648
The world slowed to a sixtieth of its speed. The
muzzle flash of the first rifle blossomed

3169
04:16:05,668 --> 04:16:11,502
in the dark like a slow yellow flower. He could see
the bullet leave the barrel. He could

3170
04:16:11,522 --> 04:16:17,354
see the young man's face behind it — terror, and
exhaustion, and, beneath both, a flicker

3171
04:16:17,375 --> 04:16:21,235
of confusion that he was already too
late to finish having.

3172
04:16:21,804 --> 04:16:27,656
He stepped to the left. It took, in subjective time,
about two seconds. In the time of

3173
04:16:27,676 --> 04:16:30,524
the room, it did not take any time at all.

3174
04:16:31,494 --> 04:16:35,655
The first man died without understanding
that anyone had moved.

3175
04:16:36,225 --> 04:16:42,045
The second was thrown. He hit the door of a freezer
from the nineteen-nineties hard enough

3176
04:16:42,065 --> 04:16:44,056
to fold it, and did not get up.

3177
04:16:44,626 --> 04:16:50,500
The third — the boy from Tennessee — got off one more
shot, which went into the ceiling,

3178
04:16:50,520 --> 04:16:56,192
before a gloved hand closed on his rifle and bent
the barrel ninety degrees, and then

3179
04:16:56,211 --> 04:16:57,599
closed on his throat.

3180
04:16:59,668 --> 04:17:00,933
The fourth ran.

3181
04:17:01,504 --> 04:17:07,054
He was the oldest of them, and the most experienced,
and he had seen enough in the dark

3182
04:17:07,074 --> 04:17:12,752
of the basement to understand, instantly and
completely, that nothing he had been trained

3183
04:17:12,772 --> 04:17:18,193
for was going to help him. He ran up the ramp, and
out through the loading dock door,

3184
04:17:18,213 --> 04:17:23,123
and across the parking lot, toward the van,
toward the road, toward anything.

3185
04:17:23,692 --> 04:17:30,051
It was twelve fifty-six. The parking lot was empty.
The access road beyond it was not.

3186
04:17:32,121 --> 04:17:38,274
A woman named Keisha Bell was driving north on the
access road at that moment, in a grey

3187
04:17:38,295 --> 04:17:44,658
Toyota, taking a rideshare passenger home from a bar
in Trolley Square. She had a dashboard

3188
04:17:44,679 --> 04:17:51,183
camera, because she drove for a living and had been
rear-ended twice. It recorded everything,

3189
04:17:51,202 --> 04:17:53,988
all the time, at thirty frames a second.

3190
04:17:54,558 --> 04:18:00,309
It recorded a man in dark clothing sprinting across
the Umbrella parking lot, from left

3191
04:18:00,329 --> 04:18:02,566
to right, under the sodium lights.

3192
04:18:03,136 --> 04:18:05,335
And then it recorded something else.

3193
04:18:07,405 --> 04:18:12,941
Frame one thousand one hundred and ten: the man,
running, forty metres from the loading

3194
04:18:12,960 --> 04:18:18,368
dock door. Frame one thousand one hundred and nine:
a shape at the loading dock door.

3195
04:18:18,388 --> 04:18:23,923
Frame one thousand one hundred and twelve: a dark
smear, long and horizontal, stretched

3196
04:18:23,942 --> 04:18:29,478
across a third of the parking lot, like a shadow
that had been torn. Frame one thousand

3197
04:18:29,498 --> 04:18:35,097
one hundred and thirteen: the smear, longer. Frame
one thousand one hundred and fourteen

3198
04:18:35,118 --> 04:18:35,161
—

3199
04:18:36,730 --> 04:18:42,306
Frame one thousand one hundred and fourteen showed a
tall figure in a long black coat,

3200
04:18:42,326 --> 04:18:47,965
standing perfectly still beside the running man,
with one hand on the back of his neck.

3201
04:18:48,537 --> 04:18:54,034
In frame one thousand one hundred and fifteen, the
running man was on the ground. The

3202
04:18:54,055 --> 04:18:57,541
figure in the coat was looking
directly at the camera.

3203
04:19:00,110 --> 04:19:05,791
Keisha Bell did not see any of it. She was watching
the road. Her passenger was asleep.

3204
04:19:05,811 --> 04:19:11,755
The car went on, north, toward the city, and neither
of them knew that they had just driven

3205
04:19:11,774 --> 04:19:14,835
past the end of the world they
had grown up in.

3206
04:19:17,404 --> 04:19:23,242
He stood in the parking lot for a long time, looking
at the tail lights of the grey Toyota

3207
04:19:23,262 --> 04:19:24,478
disappearing north.

3208
04:19:25,048 --> 04:19:31,673
He knew about dashcams. Tomás had taught him about
dashcams in the autumn of twenty twenty-six,

3209
04:19:31,692 --> 04:19:36,569
in a break room, with a phone. He knew
exactly what that car had seen.

3210
04:19:37,138 --> 04:19:42,980
There was no fixing it. He had already run through the
possibilities, in the Thought Acceleration,

3211
04:19:43,001 --> 04:19:48,364
in the time it had taken the car to pass. He could
find the car. He could find the driver.

3212
04:19:48,385 --> 04:19:53,567
He could make the driver and the passenger and the
footage disappear. And that would be

3213
04:19:53,587 --> 04:19:59,251
five bodies instead of four, and two missing persons,
and a rideshare company asking questions,

3214
04:19:59,271 --> 04:20:04,754
and a pattern — and a pattern was the one thing he had
spent three years never, ever leaving

3215
04:20:04,773 --> 04:20:05,173
behind.

3216
04:20:06,863 --> 04:20:09,998
Four. In forty seconds. Did you feel it?

3217
04:20:10,519 --> 04:20:12,674
I felt a very expensive mistake.

3218
04:20:13,193 --> 04:20:18,809
You felt what you are. For three years you have
hidden it behind contracts and laminated

3219
04:20:18,829 --> 04:20:24,190
cards. Tonight you were what you are. I have been
waiting for that since the bunker.

3220
04:20:24,710 --> 04:20:30,046
Tonight, a frightened boy killed the only man in
this company who could have sent me to

3221
04:20:30,066 --> 04:20:35,278
prison, and I killed four men in front of a camera.
That isn't what I am. That's what

3222
04:20:35,298 --> 04:20:37,064
I'm going to have to explain.

3223
04:20:39,084 --> 04:20:44,809
He took out his phone. And Albert Wesker — who had
come into this world determined to

3224
04:20:44,829 --> 04:20:50,895
be seen by no one — did the one thing that the Adolf
half of the mind had never, in either

3225
04:20:50,915 --> 04:20:53,262
of their lives, expected him to do.

3226
04:20:53,831 --> 04:20:55,550
He called the police himself.

3227
04:20:57,620 --> 04:21:04,135
By two-forty in the morning there were seven police
vehicles in the parking lot, two ambulances,

3228
04:21:04,155 --> 04:21:10,465
a coroner's van, and a Delaware State Police helicopter
circling overhead with a searchlight.

3229
04:21:11,036 --> 04:21:16,332
The Director was waiting for them in the lobby of
the old building, in his black coat,

3230
04:21:16,352 --> 04:21:21,585
with his sunglasses on. There was no blood on him.
There was not a hair out of place.

3231
04:21:22,156 --> 04:21:28,299
He had told the dispatcher, calmly and in full
sentences, exactly what had happened. Armed

3232
04:21:28,319 --> 04:21:34,396
intruders. A senior scientist shot dead. Four
intruders dead. He had not mentioned speed.

3233
04:21:34,416 --> 04:21:40,423
He had not needed to. Four men with rifles, and one
unarmed chief executive who was fit,

3234
04:21:40,442 --> 04:21:45,902
and trained, and very lucky, and had defended
himself and his staff in the dark.

3235
04:21:46,470 --> 04:21:50,281
That was the story. It would be the
story for several days.

3236
04:21:50,851 --> 04:21:56,728
Owen Carrick arrived at three-fifteen, in a coat
thrown over a T-shirt, having driven

3237
04:21:56,747 --> 04:22:03,040
from Media at a hundred miles an hour after the call
from the Bureau's night desk — because

3238
04:22:03,059 --> 04:22:09,282
the dead scientist was his confidential human source,
and the dead intruders were carrying

3239
04:22:09,302 --> 04:22:12,335
rifles, and the Bureau was
taking the scene.

3240
04:22:12,906 --> 04:22:18,631
He saw Lessing first. On the basement floor, against
the freezers, under a sheet. Somebody

3241
04:22:18,652 --> 04:22:24,122
lifted the sheet for him. The old man's face was
peaceful. Carrick would remember that

3242
04:22:24,143 --> 04:22:27,316
for the rest of his life, and
never understand it.

3243
04:22:29,385 --> 04:22:34,538
Then he went up to the lobby, and found the Director
waiting for him, standing among the

3244
04:22:34,558 --> 04:22:36,713
police as though he had invited them.

3245
04:22:37,403 --> 04:22:38,403
Agent Carrick.

3246
04:22:38,923 --> 04:22:44,748
He said it with great gentleness. He said it the way
you say something to a man at a funeral.

3247
04:22:45,440 --> 04:22:47,361
I'm so sorry about your informant.

3248
04:22:49,880 --> 04:22:55,413
Carrick did not say anything. He could not. He stood
in the lobby with the red and blue

3249
04:22:55,433 --> 04:23:00,965
lights moving across both their faces, and he
understood — completely, and all at once,

3250
04:23:00,985 --> 04:23:06,516
the way you understand that you have been falling
for a long time only when you hit the

3251
04:23:06,537 --> 04:23:12,132
ground — that the man in front of him had known about
Howard Lessing from the very first

3252
04:23:12,153 --> 04:23:12,388
day.

3253
04:23:12,959 --> 04:23:18,466
Had known, and had let it happen. Had let him wear
the wire. Had fed him three months

3254
04:23:18,486 --> 04:23:21,003
of liquid nitrogen and healthy monkeys.

3255
04:23:21,572 --> 04:23:27,365
And was telling him so now, in front of a dozen
police officers, in a voice so kind that

3256
04:23:27,386 --> 04:23:31,989
no one else in the room would ever hear it
as anything but condolence.

3257
04:23:34,810 --> 04:23:38,290
Assessment. Deniability compromised.

3258
04:23:43,750 --> 04:23:49,420
It was the first time the System had ever taken
anything away from him. He read that line

3259
04:23:49,440 --> 04:23:55,429
for a long time, standing in the lobby, with the police
moving around him and the helicopter's

3260
04:23:55,449 --> 04:23:57,538
searchlight sweeping the windows.

3261
04:23:58,859 --> 04:24:01,685
Purchase confirmed. Immunity.

3262
04:24:03,748 --> 04:24:08,951
He bought the immunity that night, for no reason he
could have explained, except that

3263
04:24:08,971 --> 04:24:13,192
a man who is going to be on television
should be difficult to poison.

3264
04:24:15,263 --> 04:24:20,899
Keisha Bell's passenger woke up on Monday
afternoon, and saw the news, and remembered

3265
04:24:20,920 --> 04:24:26,956
that the driver had a dashcam, and asked her,
half-joking, whether she had caught anything.

3266
04:24:27,525 --> 04:24:34,036
She had. She posted it on Monday night, with the
caption: wtf did my dashcam catch last

3267
04:24:34,056 --> 04:24:40,490
night outside that umbrella place. By Tuesday
morning it had been watched nine million

3268
04:24:40,511 --> 04:24:40,941
times.

3269
04:24:41,512 --> 04:24:47,494
The internet called it the ghost video. And in a dark
office in Philadelphia, at ten past

3270
04:24:47,513 --> 04:24:53,227
five on Tuesday morning, Owen Carrick sat with a
cold cup of coffee and watched frame

3271
04:24:53,246 --> 04:24:57,612
one thousand one hundred and fourteen for
the two hundredth time.

3272
04:24:58,181 --> 04:25:04,131
A figure in a long black coat. Standing perfectly
still. Forty metres from the door it

3273
04:25:04,152 --> 04:25:10,031
had been standing at, four frames — a hundred and
thirty-three milliseconds — before.

3274
04:25:10,603 --> 04:25:16,420
Forty metres in a hundred and thirty-three
milliseconds. He had done the arithmetic on

3275
04:25:16,440 --> 04:25:22,256
a legal pad twenty-two times. It came to three
hundred metres a second. Just under the

3276
04:25:22,277 --> 04:25:23,275
speed of sound.

3277
04:25:25,344 --> 04:25:31,109
Owen Carrick had spent seventeen years building a
career on the principle that there is

3278
04:25:31,129 --> 04:25:36,825
always a rational explanation. He had built it
carefully, one promotion at a time, and

3279
04:25:36,844 --> 04:25:42,941
he had just been passed over for the job he wanted, and
his informant was dead on a basement

3280
04:25:42,960 --> 04:25:48,856
floor, and he was looking at a frame of video that no
rational explanation could survive.

3281
04:25:49,426 --> 04:25:55,683
He did not close the file. He never would. That
morning, at ten past five, in the dark,

3282
04:25:55,703 --> 04:25:59,509
Special Agent Owen Carrick stopped
being a careerist.

3283
04:26:00,078 --> 04:26:05,505
He became something else. Something the Bureau did
not have a word for, and would spend

3284
04:26:05,525 --> 04:26:08,762
the next sixteen years trying and
failing to manage.

3285
04:26:25,282 --> 04:26:30,382
For fourteen days, the ghost video was the most
watched piece of footage on Earth.

3286
04:26:30,952 --> 04:26:37,265
It was slowed down, and sped up, and enhanced, and set
to music, and analysed by physicists

3287
04:26:37,286 --> 04:26:43,945
on podcasts and by teenagers in bedrooms. A man in
Tennessee who ran a channel about unexplained

3288
04:26:43,966 --> 04:26:49,930
phenomena put out a two-hour video on frame one
thousand one hundred and fourteen, and

3289
04:26:49,949 --> 04:26:55,914
it was watched eighteen million times. A professor
of biomechanics at Stanford gave an

3290
04:26:55,934 --> 04:27:01,898
interview in which she said, carefully, that no
human being could accelerate a body to

3291
04:27:01,918 --> 04:27:07,952
three hundred metres a second without being killed
by the acceleration itself, and that

3292
04:27:07,972 --> 04:27:12,057
she had therefore concluded that the
video had been edited.

3293
04:27:12,628 --> 04:27:19,280
It had not been edited. The Bureau's own forensic
laboratory in Quantico established that

3294
04:27:19,300 --> 04:27:26,103
within forty-eight hours — that the file on Keisha
Bell's dashcam memory card was original,

3295
04:27:26,123 --> 04:27:28,054
unaltered, and continuous.

3296
04:27:28,623 --> 04:27:34,824
The Bureau's laboratory also established, in the
same report, that the smear in frames

3297
04:27:34,843 --> 04:27:41,841
eleven-twelve and eleven-thirteen was consistent with,
quote, a known artefact of rolling-shutter

3298
04:27:41,860 --> 04:27:48,278
CMOS sensors when a high-contrast object crosses a
low-light scene, and that the apparent

3299
04:27:48,297 --> 04:27:52,402
velocity could not be reliably
inferred from the footage.

3300
04:27:52,971 --> 04:27:58,792
