04:44:41,287 --> 04:44:47,599
of Health and Human Services in August. It was still
there in October. It was still there

3501
04:44:47,618 --> 04:44:53,790
in December. It was never rejected. It was simply
never read, by a department that had,

3502
04:44:53,810 --> 04:44:59,979
that summer, accepted a three-hundred-million-dollar
grant from the Umbrella Foundation

3503
04:45:00,000 --> 04:45:06,171
to build rural health clinics in six states, and was
planning a ribbon-cutting with the

3504
04:45:06,190 --> 04:45:07,949
Secretary for the spring.

3505
04:45:08,519 --> 04:45:14,863
Nobody had told the deputy assistant secretary to sit
on it. That was the art of it. Nobody

3506
04:45:14,883 --> 04:45:20,806
ever needs to be told. They only need to be able to
imagine what might happen if they

3507
04:45:20,826 --> 04:45:21,295
didn't.

3508
04:45:23,485 --> 04:45:30,070
You have made a policeman into a joke and a doctor
into an unread file. Without a single

3509
04:45:30,091 --> 04:45:30,596
threat.

3510
04:45:31,117 --> 04:45:33,465
Threats leave fingerprints. Budgets don't.

3511
04:45:33,985 --> 04:45:37,228
In my day, we would simply have
arrested them.

3512
04:45:37,748 --> 04:45:43,551
In your day, the people you arrested became martyrs,
and the martyrs became a resistance,

3513
04:45:43,572 --> 04:45:49,570
and the resistance became a problem you had to solve
with more arrests. I prefer my problems

3514
04:45:49,591 --> 04:45:50,880
to solve themselves.

3515
04:45:52,900 --> 04:45:57,493
In the middle of it all, almost as an
afterthought, Aeterna was approved.

3516
04:45:58,062 --> 04:46:04,171
The Food and Drug Administration granted full
approval on the eighteenth of June, twenty

3517
04:46:04,190 --> 04:46:10,161
thirty, for the treatment of age-related frailty in
adults over sixty-five. It was the

3518
04:46:10,181 --> 04:46:16,567
fastest approval of a new molecular entity in the
agency's history. At the press conference,

3519
04:46:16,587 --> 04:46:22,975
Arthur Kowalczyk, eighty-one years old, stood up in the
front row, and when the Commissioner

3520
04:46:22,995 --> 04:46:29,104
said the word approved, he punched the air like a man
at a ball game, and the photograph

3521
04:46:29,125 --> 04:46:35,025
of him doing it was on the front page of every
newspaper in America the next morning.

3522
04:46:35,595 --> 04:46:42,039
Within a year, Aeterna would be prescribed to four
million Americans. Within three, forty

3523
04:46:42,059 --> 04:46:48,649
million people worldwide. It would reduce hospital
admissions among the elderly by a third.

3524
04:46:48,669 --> 04:46:54,677
It would be, by any honest measure, one of the
most beneficial medicines ever made.

3525
04:46:55,246 --> 04:47:01,075
That is the part of this story that is hardest to
hold in your mind at the same time as

3526
04:47:01,095 --> 04:47:07,122
the rest of it. So hold it anyway. It was true.
Millions of people lived longer, healthier

3527
04:47:07,143 --> 04:47:11,021
lives because of a man who did not
care about them at all.

3528
04:47:13,841 --> 04:47:17,368
Assessment. Market dominance, global.

3529
04:47:21,565 --> 04:47:22,997
Level twenty-six.

3530
04:47:27,057 --> 04:47:30,188
Purchase confirmed. Memory palace.

3531
04:47:33,926 --> 04:47:39,637
The Memory Palace had to be built. The System said
so, in a line of small text beneath

3532
04:47:39,657 --> 04:47:45,302
the purchase — the structure is determined by the
Host — and so, that night, lying on

3533
04:47:45,323 --> 04:47:51,234
his back in the dark in the bare apartment on the
riverfront where he slept perhaps three

3534
04:47:51,254 --> 04:47:53,032
hours a night, he built it.

3535
04:47:53,602 --> 04:47:57,689
It was the first real argument they had
had about architecture.

3536
04:47:59,378 --> 04:48:05,806
A palace should be a palace. Columns. Marble. A
great hall. A chancellery. Rooms that

3537
04:48:05,826 --> 04:48:08,915
make the men who walk in them feel small.

3538
04:48:09,435 --> 04:48:10,779
It's a filing system.

3539
04:48:11,299 --> 04:48:16,955
It is the inside of your mind. You will live in it
more than you live in your body. Make

3540
04:48:16,974 --> 04:48:19,664
it worthy of what you are going to become.

3541
04:48:21,383 --> 04:48:27,341
Wesker did not answer at once. And when he did, it
was not an argument. It was simply

3542
04:48:27,361 --> 04:48:32,191
a decision, made quietly, the way he made
all the ones that mattered.

3543
04:48:32,761 --> 04:48:34,885
He built it as the old building.

3544
04:48:35,454 --> 04:48:41,974
Four storeys of tired brick. A lobby with a water
stain. A break room with a humming vending

3545
04:48:41,994 --> 04:48:48,090
machine and an old piano in the corner. A corner
office with a leather chair. And then

3546
04:48:48,110 --> 04:48:54,346
— because it was a mind, and minds do not have to
obey geology — corridors that went on,

3547
04:48:54,366 --> 04:49:00,815
and on, beyond the walls, down stairs that did not
exist, into floors beneath the basement,

3548
04:49:00,835 --> 04:49:07,001
each corridor lined with doors, and behind every
door, a person. Every face he had ever

3549
04:49:07,021 --> 04:49:10,699
read. Every file. Every
conversation, word for word.

3550
04:49:11,269 --> 04:49:16,974
Walter Pruitt had a door on the ground floor, by
the security booth. Hannah Price had

3551
04:49:16,994 --> 04:49:21,153
one in the basement, beside a freezer.
Owen Carrick had three.

3552
04:49:21,843 --> 04:49:23,655
You made it the building.

3553
04:49:24,175 --> 04:49:29,316
It's the only place I've ever started from nothing.
I'd like to remember what that was

3554
04:49:29,336 --> 04:49:29,617
like.

3555
04:49:31,637 --> 04:49:37,592
The Adolf half of the mind said nothing for a long
time. And then, very quietly, in a

3556
04:49:37,612 --> 04:49:43,847
room on the fourth floor of an imaginary building
that only one skull contained, it asked

3557
04:49:43,867 --> 04:49:49,819
for a door of its own. And Wesker, after a moment,
gave it one — on the fourth floor,

3558
04:49:49,840 --> 04:49:53,474
at the end of a corridor, with no
name on it at all.

3559
04:49:55,545 --> 04:50:01,438
On the second of September, at seven in the morning,
a black SUV with government plates

3560
04:50:01,457 --> 04:50:06,264
pulled into the visitors' bay of the
Umbrella tower, and a man got out.

3561
04:50:06,833 --> 04:50:12,363
He was lean, somewhere in his late fifties, with
silver hair and a navy suit and no tie,

3562
04:50:12,383 --> 04:50:17,786
and the kind of face that has spent a long career
being forgettable on purpose. He did

3563
04:50:17,806 --> 04:50:23,272
not have an appointment. He walked into the lobby,
past the living wall, past the Creed

3564
04:50:23,292 --> 04:50:28,693
in its small elegant letters, to the reception desk,
where a receptionist who had once

3565
04:50:28,713 --> 04:50:33,801
been a Common roll in a basement ten-pull looked
up at him with perfect courtesy.

3566
04:50:34,492 --> 04:50:39,158
Good morning. My name's Martin Sloane. I'd like to
see Doctor Wesker, please. Tell him

3567
04:50:39,179 --> 04:50:40,467
I'm from the government.

3568
04:50:41,466 --> 04:50:42,680
The other part of it.

3569
04:50:44,699 --> 04:50:50,328
The receptionist did not ask which part. She did
not need to. She picked up a phone.

3570
04:50:50,897 --> 04:50:57,050
On the twelfth floor, in a corner office with a view of
the river, Albert Wesker had watched

3571
04:50:57,070 --> 04:51:02,887
the black SUV arrive on the security feed. He had
watched the man get out, and walk in,

3572
04:51:02,907 --> 04:51:08,592
and put the card on the desk. And the Cold-Read,
even through a security camera, even

3573
04:51:08,612 --> 04:51:14,429
from twelve floors away, had shown him everything he
needed to know about Martin Sloane

3574
04:51:14,449 --> 04:51:15,368
in one glance.

3575
04:51:15,939 --> 04:51:21,974
Curiosity. Ambition. Contempt for rules. And
beneath it all, like the steady light of

3576
04:51:21,993 --> 04:51:28,099
a pilot flame, something he had not seen in any of
the three. Something he recognised,

3577
04:51:28,119 --> 04:51:31,377
because he saw it every morning
in the mirror.

3578
04:51:31,948 --> 04:51:33,001
Appetite.

3579
04:51:49,444 --> 04:51:55,005
Martin Sloane did not waste time. It was the first
thing the Director liked about him.

3580
04:51:55,693 --> 04:52:00,738
I'm not here to investigate you, Doctor Wesker. I
want to be clear about that. The Bureau

3581
04:52:00,758 --> 04:52:05,573
does that. The Bureau is very bad at it, as you've
noticed. My part of the government

3582
04:52:05,593 --> 04:52:07,450
doesn't investigate. It collects.

3583
04:52:07,970 --> 04:52:09,274
And what are you collecting?

3584
04:52:09,792 --> 04:52:13,823
Information. About people who are about to try
to take something from you.

3585
04:52:15,224 --> 04:52:20,661
Since January, we've been hearing a word in some very
interesting places. In Moscow, mostly.

3586
04:52:20,681 --> 04:52:25,704
In some traffic between people who work for a
certain service, and people who used to

3587
04:52:25,724 --> 04:52:30,746
work for a certain private military company that
the Kremlin likes to pretend doesn't

3588
04:52:30,766 --> 04:52:35,790
exist. The word is Umbrella. Sometimes they also
use a word that translates, roughly,

3589
04:52:35,810 --> 04:52:36,323
as ghost.

3590
04:52:36,844 --> 04:52:38,028
They've seen the video.

3591
04:52:38,547 --> 04:52:44,029
Everybody's seen the video. The difference is that some
people watched it and saw a rolling-shutter

3592
04:52:44,048 --> 04:52:47,029
artefact, and some people watched it
and saw a weapon.

3593
04:52:48,548 --> 04:52:50,257
And which did you see, Mister Sloane?

3594
04:52:52,278 --> 04:52:58,447
Sloane smiled. It was a thin, cold, genuinely
amused smile, and the Cold-Read read it

3595
04:52:58,467 --> 04:53:00,850
with something close to pleasure.

3596
04:53:01,539 --> 04:53:06,197
I saw a man who doesn't want to be collected by the
Russians. I'm here to suggest that

3597
04:53:06,217 --> 04:53:08,101
you'd prefer to be collected by us.

3598
04:53:09,619 --> 04:53:15,282
He left a telephone number on a plain white card,
and a warning, and nothing else. He

3599
04:53:15,302 --> 04:53:19,027
did not ask for anything. That, too,
the Director liked.

3600
04:53:21,218 --> 04:53:23,621
The Americans want to own you.

3601
04:53:24,140 --> 04:53:29,315
The Americans want to rent me. There's a difference.
And the Russians want to steal me.

3602
04:53:29,335 --> 04:53:34,391
Which means that, for the first time, someone is
going to come at us who isn't afraid

3603
04:53:34,412 --> 04:53:35,227
of the police.

3604
04:53:35,747 --> 04:53:37,370
Then let them come.

3605
04:53:37,888 --> 04:53:39,165
Yes. I think we should.

3606
04:53:40,686 --> 04:53:43,338
You are thinking of the creature.

3607
04:53:43,858 --> 04:53:49,512
I'm thinking that I own a weapon I've never seen used.
I'm thinking that a Russian contractor

3608
04:53:49,532 --> 04:53:54,883
team is the only enemy on Earth I could use it on
that nobody would ever report missing.

3609
04:53:54,903 --> 04:54:00,252
And I'm thinking that it's been asleep for four
years, and I'd like to know what it does

3610
04:54:00,272 --> 04:54:01,289
when it wakes up.

3611
04:54:03,309 --> 04:54:09,223
The Adolf half of the mind was silent for a long
moment. When it spoke, its voice had

3612
04:54:09,243 --> 04:54:15,226
changed — lower, slower, and with something in it
that the Wesker half had never quite

3613
04:54:15,245 --> 04:54:19,065
heard before. Not joy. Something
more careful than joy.

3614
04:54:19,756 --> 04:54:25,702
You have always told me to wait. Not yet. Not yet.
You told the girl at the piano. You

3615
04:54:25,722 --> 04:54:27,713
told the creature in the ice.

3616
04:54:28,234 --> 04:54:28,939
I did.

3617
04:54:29,458 --> 04:54:30,547
And now?

3618
04:54:31,068 --> 04:54:32,882
Now it's a controlled experiment.

3619
04:54:35,532 --> 04:54:38,833
Purchase confirmed. Desert complex.

3620
04:54:43,708 --> 04:54:49,470
The facility appeared in the Mojave Desert in the
second week of October, forty miles

3621
04:54:49,490 --> 04:54:55,252
from the nearest town, on land that an Ashford
Pacific subsidiary had bought from the

3622
04:54:55,272 --> 04:54:59,197
Bureau of Land Management two years
before and never used.

3623
04:54:59,768 --> 04:55:06,097
On the surface, it was a data centre. Low concrete
buildings, a fence, a row of cooling

3624
04:55:06,117 --> 04:55:12,372
units, a microwave mast. It even held data — real
servers, running real workloads, for

3625
04:55:12,393 --> 04:55:18,649
real customers, at a loss. Below the surface, four
levels down, it was something else.

3626
04:55:19,220 --> 04:55:24,979
They moved the Tyrant out of Wilmington on a moonless
night, in a refrigerated truck with

3627
04:55:25,000 --> 04:55:30,502
the name of a produce company on its side, escorted
by four of the Silent Ones in two

3628
04:55:30,522 --> 04:55:36,087
black cars, across two thousand four hundred miles
of interstate. Evelyn Marsh rode in

3629
04:55:36,108 --> 04:55:41,804
the cab. She had insisted. She had not left the
cradle's side for four years and she was

3630
04:55:41,824 --> 04:55:44,856
not, she told the Director,
going to start now.

3631
04:55:45,426 --> 04:55:51,593
It arrived on the third day, and was lowered into the
deepest level of the desert complex,

3632
04:55:51,613 --> 04:55:55,441
and bolted down. Its breathing, in
the new room, echoed.

3633
04:55:57,512 --> 04:56:01,705
And then — carefully, over three weeks — he
let the Russians find it.

3634
04:56:02,275 --> 04:56:07,940
It was not difficult. Ashford Pacific's logistics
system had been under quiet attack from

3635
04:56:07,960 --> 04:56:13,628
a group in Saint Petersburg since the spring; Hale's
people had noticed in April and done

3636
04:56:13,648 --> 04:56:19,186
nothing, on the Director's instructions, except
watch. Now, in the shipping records the

3637
04:56:19,206 --> 04:56:25,256
hackers were reading, a single line appeared. A transfer
of biological material, classification

3638
04:56:25,276 --> 04:56:30,942
restricted, from Wilmington to a data centre in
Nevada. Handling instructions: cryogenic.

3639
04:56:30,962 --> 04:56:32,349
Security escort: four.

3640
04:56:32,919 --> 04:56:36,366
Four. It was a very small number.
It was meant to be.

3641
04:56:38,436 --> 04:56:43,158
They came on the nineteenth of November, at four
minutes past two in the morning.

3642
04:56:43,729 --> 04:56:49,327
There were twelve of them. They had come into the
country separately, over two weeks,

3643
04:56:49,347 --> 04:56:55,208
on real passports, through four different airports,
and assembled in a rented ranch house

3644
04:56:55,228 --> 04:57:01,156
outside Barstow. They were former soldiers of the
Russian special forces, and of a private

3645
04:57:01,176 --> 04:57:07,104
company that had fought in Syria, and Libya, and Mali,
and Ukraine. They had night vision,

3646
04:57:07,125 --> 04:57:13,185
and suppressed rifles, and a drone, and a thermal lance
for the vault door, and a helicopter

3647
04:57:13,205 --> 04:57:18,868
waiting fifteen miles away with a pilot who had
flown for three different governments.

3648
04:57:19,439 --> 04:57:24,882
They were, by any standard, the most dangerous group
of people who had ever tried to take

3649
04:57:24,902 --> 04:57:26,601
anything from Albert Wesker.

3650
04:57:27,170 --> 04:57:33,387
He watched them come, from a control room four levels
down, on a wall of screens. Evelyn

3651
04:57:33,406 --> 04:57:36,292
stood beside him. Hale stood by the door.

3652
04:57:36,981 --> 04:57:39,949
Fence is down. Two guards at the
east door. Moving.

3653
04:57:40,468 --> 04:57:46,137
The two guards at the east door were Silent Ones.
They had been told to step aside. They

3654
04:57:46,156 --> 04:57:51,954
stepped aside, with perfect courtesy, and the twelve
men went past them without ever quite

3655
04:57:51,974 --> 04:57:54,862
seeing them, and into the
building, and down.

3656
04:57:56,932 --> 04:58:02,673
At the fourth level, in front of a steel door with a
red-and-white emblem painted on it,

3657
04:58:02,693 --> 04:58:05,949
the team leader raised his hand,
and they stopped.

3658
04:58:06,518 --> 04:58:11,476
And four levels down, in the control room,
Albert Wesker said two words.

3659
04:58:13,169 --> 04:58:13,895
Wake it.

3660
04:58:16,415 --> 04:58:22,275
In the deepest room of the desert complex, for the
first time in four years, the cradle

3661
04:58:22,295 --> 04:58:22,748
opened.

3662
04:58:25,818 --> 04:58:27,861
The team saw it first on thermal.

3663
04:58:28,549 --> 04:58:32,731
Contact. Contact, front. Something big. Very
hot. Very — it's very big.

3664
04:58:33,131 --> 04:58:34,156
Is that a man?

3665
04:58:35,557 --> 04:58:36,601
That's not a man.

3666
04:58:37,620 --> 04:58:43,311
They opened fire. They were professionals, and they
did it well — four rifles at once,

3667
04:58:43,331 --> 04:58:49,153
controlled bursts, aimed at the centre of mass. The
corridor filled with light and noise

3668
04:58:49,172 --> 04:58:50,813
and the smell of cordite.

3669
04:58:51,383 --> 04:58:57,729
It did not stop. It did not hurry, either. It walked
into the gunfire the way a man walks

3670
04:58:57,750 --> 04:58:58,445
into rain.

3671
04:59:00,515 --> 04:59:05,969
What happened in the next ninety seconds, the
Director watched on the wall of screens

3672
04:59:05,989 --> 04:59:11,570
with his arms folded, and Evelyn Marsh watched
beside him with a notebook, writing, and

3673
04:59:11,591 --> 04:59:15,950
Hale watched by the door, and none of the
three of them looked away.

3674
04:59:16,520 --> 04:59:22,191
The narrator will not describe it. Not because it
cannot be described — but because the

3675
04:59:22,211 --> 04:59:27,885
men in that corridor were somebody's sons, and they
had come to steal, and they did not

3676
04:59:27,904 --> 04:59:33,512
deserve to be remembered only as the first people on
this Earth to learn what a Tyrant

3677
04:59:33,531 --> 04:59:34,034
was for.

3678
04:59:37,604 --> 04:59:41,923
It took ninety seconds. The radio chatter
lasted seventy of them.

3679
04:59:42,614 --> 04:59:44,907
Fall back! Fall back to the — it's
in the — oh God —

3680
04:59:47,426 --> 04:59:48,705
And then it didn't.

3681
04:59:50,775 --> 04:59:57,019
Twenty miles away, a helicopter pilot who had flown for
three different governments listened

3682
04:59:57,039 --> 05:00:03,078
to his radio go silent, and waited exactly as long as
his training said to wait, and then

3683
05:00:03,098 --> 05:00:08,865
lifted off, alone, and flew south toward the
Mexican border, and was never heard from

3684
05:00:08,885 --> 05:00:11,384
again by anyone who had employed him.

3685
05:00:14,454 --> 05:00:17,522
In the control room, nobody
spoke for a while.

3686
05:00:18,093 --> 05:00:23,905
Evelyn finished writing. She closed her notebook, and
held it against her chest with both

3687
05:00:23,925 --> 05:00:30,002
arms, and looked at the wall of screens, where a
nine-foot grey figure was standing perfectly

3688
05:00:30,022 --> 05:00:35,245
still at the end of a smoke-filled corridor,
waiting to be told what to do next.

3689
05:00:35,935 --> 05:00:36,886
It was perfect.

3690
05:00:38,404 --> 05:00:42,671
Hale said nothing. Hale never said
anything he had not been asked.

3691
05:00:43,242 --> 05:00:48,926
The Director stood with his arms folded, and looked
at the screen, and did not move for

3692
05:00:48,947 --> 05:00:49,712
a long time.

3693
05:00:51,904 --> 05:00:53,440
Do you see it now?

3694
05:00:53,960 --> 05:00:54,752
I see it.

3695
05:00:55,271 --> 05:01:01,633
Twelve soldiers. The best Russia has. Ninety seconds.
One of them. Do you understand what

3696
05:01:01,653 --> 05:01:04,788
we could do with a hundred?
With a thousand?

3697
05:01:05,308 --> 05:01:06,717
I understand exactly.

3698
05:01:07,237 --> 05:01:14,120
An army of these. Not in a desert. In the streets of a
capital. At the gates of a parliament.

3699
05:01:14,140 --> 05:01:20,059
No one could stand against it. No one. Not the
Americans, not the Chinese, not —

3700
05:01:20,579 --> 05:01:21,261
Stop.

3701
05:01:23,279 --> 05:01:29,132
It was the first time Wesker had ever told the other
voice to stop. It was not a request.

3702
05:01:29,822 --> 05:01:34,687
An army of these is a war. Listen to me. A war. With
the whole world. Tomorrow. Against

3703
05:01:34,707 --> 05:01:39,740
every government on this planet, all at once, united —
for the first time in their history

3704
05:01:39,760 --> 05:01:44,512
— by the one thing they will all agree on, which is
that we have to be destroyed. You

3705
05:01:44,532 --> 05:01:48,725
know how that ends. You were there. You ended
it in a bunker with a pistol.

3706
05:01:49,244 --> 05:01:51,294
I did not have this.

3707
05:01:51,814 --> 05:01:57,137
You had the best army in Europe and the world took
it apart in six years. Because they

3708
05:01:57,156 --> 05:02:02,419
saw you coming. Because you showed them. That's the
only lesson either of us was ever

3709
05:02:02,439 --> 05:02:03,537
supposed to learn.

3710
05:02:04,938 --> 05:02:10,848
We are not ready for a war. We are ready for a
market. And a market is the only war you

3711
05:02:10,868 --> 05:02:13,301
win by making your enemies grateful.

3712
05:02:15,822 --> 05:02:21,431
The old voice did not answer for a long time. When
it did, it was very quiet, and there

3713
05:02:21,451 --> 05:02:26,672
was something in it that the Wesker half of the
mind would think about for years.

3714
05:02:27,362 --> 05:02:29,126
But you enjoyed it.

3715
05:02:31,146 --> 05:02:32,812
Wesker did not answer that.

3716
05:02:33,382 --> 05:02:38,807
He looked at the screen, at the grey figure waiting
in the smoke. And he found — with

3717
05:02:38,827 --> 05:02:44,508
the precise, clinical honesty that was the best and
worst thing about him — that he could

3718
05:02:44,528 --> 05:02:46,877
not say that the old voice was wrong.

3719
05:02:49,448 --> 05:02:55,220
At a quarter past nine that morning, in a windowless
room at Langley, an analyst showed

3720
05:02:55,240 --> 05:03:01,278
Martin Sloane a sequence of thermal images from a
satellite that had passed over the Mojave

3721
05:03:01,298 --> 05:03:03,208
Desert at two in the morning.

3722
05:03:03,779 --> 05:03:10,469
Twelve small heat signatures, entering a compound. One
very large heat signature, emerging

3723
05:03:10,489 --> 05:03:17,103
from somewhere beneath it. The twelve small
signatures, one by one, fading. A helicopter,

3724
05:03:17,123 --> 05:03:18,222
leaving, alone.

3725
05:03:18,793 --> 05:03:24,383
Sloane watched the sequence four times. Then he
straightened up, and put his hands in

3726
05:03:24,403 --> 05:03:30,324
his pockets, and said, to nobody in particular, in a
voice that the analyst would describe

3727
05:03:30,344 --> 05:03:35,473
to her husband that evening as the happiest she
had ever heard her boss sound:

3728
05:03:36,163 --> 05:03:37,228
I want to meet it.

3729
05:03:39,880 --> 05:03:44,863
Assessment. Biological asset,
field validated.

3730
05:03:46,838 --> 05:03:52,506
In the desert, four levels down, the cradle closed
again. The grey figure stepped back

3731
05:03:52,526 --> 05:03:58,126
into it without being told, as if it had simply
been waiting for permission to sleep.

3732
05:03:58,694 --> 05:04:04,323
Evelyn Marsh sat on the floor of the cold room in
front of the window until morning, with

3733
05:04:04,343 --> 05:04:06,352
her palm flat against the glass.

3734
05:04:06,923 --> 05:04:13,424
There were no bodies. There was nothing to find. By
the time the sun rose over the Mojave,

3735
05:04:13,444 --> 05:04:19,583
the fourth level of the desert complex was clean,
and quiet, and smelled only faintly

3736
05:04:19,603 --> 05:04:21,033
of cordite and cold.

3737
05:04:21,601 --> 05:04:27,786
Twelve men had come to steal something. The world
never learned what had happened to them.

3738
05:04:27,806 --> 05:04:33,712
Their families, in Moscow and Rostov and Krasnodar,
were told that they had died in an

3739
05:04:33,733 --> 05:04:35,022
accident in Africa.

3740
05:04:53,281 --> 05:04:59,097
In the third week of January, in a windowless room on
the fourth floor of a green-and-cream

3741
05:04:59,116 --> 05:05:04,740
building beside the Thames, five people sat down
around a table to talk about a company.

3742
05:05:05,310 --> 05:05:10,815
None of them would ever admit that the meeting had
taken place. There was no agenda, no

3743
05:05:10,835 --> 05:05:16,976
minutes, and no name on the booking. There were only five
liaison officers from five intelligence

3744
05:05:16,996 --> 05:05:22,566
services — British, German, French, American, and
Dutch — who had each, separately, over

3745
05:05:22,586 --> 05:05:28,283
the preceding year, noticed something about Umbrella
Life Sciences that did not sit right,

3746
05:05:28,304 --> 05:05:33,556
and who had each, separately, been told by their
own governments to leave it alone.

3747
05:05:34,126 --> 05:05:39,666
The meeting was chaired by a man named Julian
Ashcombe. He was fifty-four, patrician,

3748
05:05:39,686 --> 05:05:45,224
patient, and dressed as though it were still
nineteen fifty-five. He had spent thirty

3749
05:05:45,244 --> 05:05:50,915
years in the Secret Intelligence Service, most of
them in places where patience was the

3750
05:05:50,935 --> 05:05:56,670
only weapon that did not get you killed. He was, by
the unanimous judgment of the people

3751
05:05:56,690 --> 05:06:02,491
who had worked against him, the most dangerous man in
the room, and he was always, always

3752
05:06:02,511 --> 05:06:02,950
polite.

3753
05:06:03,638 --> 05:06:05,936
Shall we begin with the patents?

3754
05:06:07,955 --> 05:06:10,612
The patents were the German contribution.

3755
05:06:11,181 --> 05:06:17,439
The Federal Intelligence Service in Berlin employed
a small unit whose only job was to

3756
05:06:17,459 --> 05:06:24,155
watch the world's patent offices for signs of
technological surprise — the sudden appearance

3757
05:06:24,175 --> 05:06:30,361
of an idea that should, by any normal measure, have
taken a decade to arrive. In four

3758
05:06:30,381 --> 05:06:36,638
years, Umbrella Life Sciences had filed four hundred
and eighteen patents. Sixty-three

3759
05:06:36,658 --> 05:06:43,574
of them, the German analysts had concluded, described
inventions with no traceable intellectual

3760
05:06:43,594 --> 05:06:50,363
ancestry. No preceding papers. No conference talks. No
doctoral theses. No failed experiments

3761
05:06:50,383 --> 05:06:56,640
by other groups. They had simply appeared, fully
formed, as if someone had been handed

3762
05:06:56,661 --> 05:06:57,516
the answers.

3763
05:06:58,206 --> 05:07:03,809
In science, ideas have parents. Sixty-three of these
are orphans. That is not statistically

3764
05:07:03,829 --> 05:07:09,121
possible. Either Umbrella has the best research team
in the history of the species, or

3765
05:07:09,141 --> 05:07:12,086
it has access to something we do
not understand.

3766
05:07:12,609 --> 05:07:15,162
Or someone else's research.

3767
05:07:15,682 --> 05:07:19,416
We looked for that first. There is no one
else. That is the problem.

3768
05:07:21,437 --> 05:07:24,056
The French contribution was the Russians.

3769
05:07:24,626 --> 05:07:29,896
The General Directorate for External Security had
been watching a particular group of

3770
05:07:29,916 --> 05:07:35,620
former Russian special forces soldiers for years,
because they had worked in French-speaking

3771
05:07:35,640 --> 05:07:41,036
Africa, and killed French-trained soldiers there. In
November, twelve of them had flown

3772
05:07:41,056 --> 05:07:46,326
into the United States on four different routes. In
December, they had not flown out.

3773
05:07:46,346 --> 05:07:51,679
Their families in Russia had been told they had died
in an accident in Mali. There had

3774
05:07:51,699 --> 05:07:53,234
been no accident in Mali.

3775
05:07:53,925 --> 05:07:58,845
Twelve men do not vanish in America. America is the
easiest country on Earth to find a

3776
05:07:58,865 --> 05:08:03,784
man in. And yet — nothing. No bodies. No arrests. No
trace. The last thing our friends

3777
05:08:03,805 --> 05:08:08,724
in Moscow were interested in, before they vanished,
was a data centre in Nevada, owned

3778
05:08:08,744 --> 05:08:11,367
by a Singapore company called
Ashford Pacific.

3779
05:08:11,887 --> 05:08:14,162
Which is owned by Umbrella.

3780
05:08:14,682 --> 05:08:16,389
Three layers down. Yes.

3781
05:08:18,408 --> 05:08:23,974
The Dutch had the ships — Ashford Pacific's cargo
vessels, which called at Rotterdam,

3782
05:08:23,993 --> 05:08:29,559
and whose manifests were meticulous, and whose
tonnage, the Dutch customs service had

3783
05:08:29,579 --> 05:08:35,144
noticed, never quite matched. The American had
nothing he was permitted to share, and

3784
05:08:35,164 --> 05:08:37,706
shared it anyway, over lunch, in a pub.

3785
05:08:38,277 --> 05:08:44,774
And Julian Ashcombe had the video. He played it once,
on the big screen, frame by frame,

3786
05:08:44,794 --> 05:08:48,623
from eleven-ten to eleven-fifteen,
in total silence.

3787
05:08:50,813 --> 05:08:55,729
So. Let me summarise, and you may correct me. A
company that did not exist five years

3788
05:08:55,750 --> 05:09:00,665
ago now dominates the most valuable medical market
on Earth. It files inventions that

3789
05:09:00,685 --> 05:09:05,775
have no parents. It builds a factory capable of four
hundred million doses of something,

3790
05:09:05,795 --> 05:09:10,826
for a drug that needs forty million. Russian special
forces go to one of its buildings,

3791
05:09:10,846 --> 05:09:15,936
and cease to exist. And its chief executive can, on
the evidence of a rideshare driver's

3792
05:09:15,956 --> 05:09:19,534
dashboard camera, cross a car park
faster than a rifle bullet.

3793
05:09:20,935 --> 05:09:25,557
Either we are looking at the most successful company
in the history of the world. Or we

3794
05:09:25,577 --> 05:09:28,705
are looking at something else, wearing
a company's clothes.

3795
05:09:30,726 --> 05:09:31,880
Which do you think?

3796
05:09:32,399 --> 05:09:36,422
I think we should find out before it
decides to take the clothes off.

3797
05:09:38,443 --> 05:09:43,586
The Director arrived in London eight days later,
for a reception at Downing Street.

3798
05:09:44,155 --> 05:09:50,037
It was an ordinary occasion, of the kind the British
government held several times a year:

3799
05:09:50,057 --> 05:09:55,675
a life sciences investment evening, with forty chief
executives, a string quartet, and

3800
05:09:55,695 --> 05:10:01,313
canapés, at which the Prime Minister would say a few
words about Britain's future as a

3801
05:10:01,333 --> 05:10:07,411
scientific superpower, and the chief executives would
each, at some point during the evening,

3802
05:10:07,431 --> 05:10:12,787
be photographed shaking the Prime Minister's hand
while announcing a large number.

3803
05:10:13,358 --> 05:10:19,347
Umbrella's number was two billion pounds — a new
research campus on the Cambridge site,

3804
05:10:19,366 --> 05:10:25,287
seventeen hundred jobs. It was announced at
eight-fifteen. At eight-sixteen, the Prime

3805
05:10:25,307 --> 05:10:31,640
Minister shook the Director's hand for the cameras, and
said something warm about Umbrella's

3806
05:10:31,661 --> 05:10:37,167
British roots, and moved on to the next chief
executive. It lasted nine seconds.

3807
05:10:37,737 --> 05:10:43,434
The Director spent the rest of the evening standing
by a window in the State Dining Room,

3808
05:10:43,454 --> 05:10:49,279
with a glass of champagne he did not drink, looking
out at the rain on Horse Guards Parade.

3809
05:10:50,849 --> 05:10:56,777
The Adolf half of the mind had been silent since the
car had turned into Whitehall. It

3810
05:10:56,797 --> 05:11:01,274
was silent for a long time. When it
spoke, its voice was strange.

3811
05:11:01,962 --> 05:11:04,184
I tried to burn this city.

3812
05:11:04,704 --> 05:11:05,387
I know.

3813
05:11:05,907 --> 05:11:11,831
Night after night. Fifty-seven nights in a row, in
the first autumn. I stood in front

3814
05:11:11,851 --> 05:11:17,917
of maps with pins in them and I chose streets. I
wanted them to break. I wanted them to

3815
05:11:17,937 --> 05:11:18,195
beg.

3816
05:11:18,716 --> 05:11:19,532
They didn't.

3817
05:11:20,054 --> 05:11:21,698
No. They did not.

3818
05:11:23,418 --> 05:11:28,558
And now their Prime Minister just shook your hand,
and thanked you for the jobs, and a

3819
05:11:28,578 --> 05:11:30,117
waiter gave you champagne.

3820
05:11:31,636 --> 05:11:33,835
Do you think that is funny?

3821
05:11:34,354 --> 05:11:39,549
I think it's the whole lesson. You came at them with
bombers, and they fought you in the

3822
05:11:39,569 --> 05:11:43,521
streets. I came with two billion pounds,
and they held a reception.

3823
05:11:45,540 --> 05:11:52,224
The Wesker half of the mind meant it as a cruelty. He
usually did. But the old voice did

3824
05:11:52,243 --> 05:11:57,328
not respond the way he expected. It did
not rage. It did not argue.

3825
05:11:57,898 --> 05:12:03,822
It simply looked out of the window at the rain on the
parade ground, through eyes it shared,

3826
05:12:03,842 --> 05:12:09,508
for a very long time, and said nothing at all. And
the Wesker half of the mind could not

3827
05:12:09,527 --> 05:12:13,773
tell — for the first time since the bunker
— what it was thinking.

3828
05:12:16,343 --> 05:12:22,283
Catherine Adeyemi had come with him. She was talking
to the chief executive of a British

3829
05:12:22,304 --> 05:12:28,244
bank about bond markets, and hating it, when a
patrician man in a dinner jacket appeared

3830
05:12:28,264 --> 05:12:32,920
at her elbow with two fresh glasses of
champagne, and handed her one.

3831
05:12:33,610 --> 05:12:39,498
Miss Adeyemi. Julian Ashcombe. We've never met. I'm
with the Foreign Office. Well — adjacent

3832
05:12:39,519 --> 05:12:39,884
to it.

3833
05:12:40,404 --> 05:12:41,484
Adjacent.

3834
05:12:42,004 --> 05:12:46,959
Very adjacent. I read about you, some years ago. The
business at Ashworth Grey. The partner,

3835
05:12:46,979 --> 05:12:51,611
and the regulator, and what happened to you
afterwards. I thought it was very brave. I

3836
05:12:51,631 --> 05:12:55,019
thought it was also very badly handled,
by everyone except you.

3837
05:12:56,538 --> 05:13:02,083
She looked at him for a long moment. Nobody had
mentioned Ashworth Grey to her in six

3838
05:13:02,103 --> 05:13:04,114
years. She had made sure of it.

3839
05:13:04,804 --> 05:13:06,661
What do you want, Mister Ashcombe?

3840
05:13:07,180 --> 05:13:12,052
Oh, nothing tonight. Tonight I only wanted to
say hello. And to give you this.

3841
05:13:12,574 --> 05:13:17,891
It was a plain white card, with a London telephone
number on it, and nothing else.

3842
05:13:18,582 --> 05:13:23,414
You once asked a very large institution a very simple
question, Miss Adeyemi. Where does

3843
05:13:23,434 --> 05:13:28,320
the money go. I've always admired people who ask that
question. If you ever find yourself

3844
05:13:28,341 --> 05:13:33,227
asking it again — about anyone — you might ring that
number. Somebody will always answer.

3845
05:13:35,247 --> 05:13:40,865
He smiled, and bowed his head very slightly, and was
gone into the crowd before she could

3846
05:13:40,885 --> 05:13:41,309
answer.

3847
05:13:42,878 --> 05:13:47,866
She should have given the card to the Director. She
told herself, on the flight home,

3848
05:13:47,886 --> 05:13:50,281
that she had meant to, and had forgotten.

3849
05:13:50,852 --> 05:13:56,377
She put it in her wallet, behind her own business
card, where it would stay, unlooked-at,

3850
05:13:56,397 --> 05:13:58,245
for two years and four months.

3851
05:13:58,815 --> 05:14:04,668
The Director knew about the card before she reached
the car. The Cold-Read had shown him

3852
05:14:04,688 --> 05:14:10,539
Ashcombe's face across the room, and hers, and the
moment the card changed hands. He did

3853
05:14:10,559 --> 05:14:12,673
not mention it. Neither did she.

3854
05:14:13,242 --> 05:14:19,082
It was the start of the longest silence between them.
It would outlast almost everything

3855
05:14:19,102 --> 05:14:19,415
else.

3856
05:14:21,986 --> 05:14:26,463
In Beijing, a woman named Wen Shulan
watched the eleven seconds.

3857
05:14:27,033 --> 05:14:32,493
She was fifty-six, a deputy minister in the Ministry
of State Security, and she had spent

3858
05:14:32,513 --> 05:14:37,974
her career in the part of it that watched foreign
technology companies. She did not watch

3859
05:14:37,993 --> 05:14:43,391
the ghost video. She had seen it, and filed it, and
set it aside. It interested her less

3860
05:14:43,412 --> 05:14:48,688
than the other one — the television interview, from
the winter of twenty twenty-eight,

3861
05:14:48,707 --> 05:14:54,351
in which a man in sunglasses had sat in silence for
eleven seconds in front of forty million

3862
05:14:54,371 --> 05:14:54,782
people.

3863
05:14:55,352 --> 05:15:01,353
She watched the eleven seconds nineteen times. She
watched the anchor's face, not his.

3864
05:15:01,373 --> 05:15:07,515
She had her analysts pull every public appearance he
had ever made and time every pause.

3865
05:15:08,206 --> 05:15:10,165
This is not a businessman.

3866
05:15:10,684 --> 05:15:12,135
Her analyst waited.

3867
05:15:12,827 --> 05:15:18,001
A businessman speaks to close a deal. This man
speaks to change the room. Every silence

3868
05:15:18,021 --> 05:15:23,613
is placed. Every sentence lands on the beat. I have
heard this before. Not from a businessman.

3869
05:15:23,633 --> 05:15:25,045
From — older recordings.

3870
05:15:26,564 --> 05:15:32,515
She did not say whose recordings. She was a careful
woman. But that afternoon she opened

3871
05:15:32,536 --> 05:15:38,350
a new file, with a new codename, at a level of
classification her service reserved for

3872
05:15:38,370 --> 05:15:39,708
foreign governments.

3873
05:15:40,277 --> 05:15:46,005
She called it Umbrella. She saw no reason to be
clever about it. She intended to take

3874
05:15:46,025 --> 05:15:47,222
it very seriously.

3875
05:15:49,294 --> 05:15:55,510
And in Moscow, in an office at the Lubyanka with a
view of a snow-covered square, a colonel

3876
05:15:55,530 --> 05:16:01,885
of the Federal Security Service named Arkady Belov
stood at his window and looked at nothing.

3877
05:16:02,456 --> 05:16:07,849
He had sent twelve men to Nevada. He had known
eight of them personally. He had drunk

3878
05:16:07,869 --> 05:16:13,326
at the weddings of three. He had told their families
about Mali himself, in person, in

3879
05:16:13,346 --> 05:16:18,485
their kitchens, and he had looked their mothers
in the eye while he lied to them.

3880
05:16:19,055 --> 05:16:24,685
Belov was not a sentimental man. He had done a great
many things in his career that he

3881
05:16:24,705 --> 05:16:30,334
did not think about. But he thought about the
twelve. He had the photograph of them on

3882
05:16:30,354 --> 05:16:33,358
his desk, and he thought about
them every day.

3883
05:16:34,047 --> 05:16:40,130
Find out what he is. Whatever it costs. And when you
have found out — tell me how to kill

3884
05:16:40,150 --> 05:16:40,335
it.

3885
05:16:42,855 --> 05:16:48,316
On the flight home, over the Atlantic, the Director
sat in the dark and thought about

3886
05:16:48,336 --> 05:16:49,155
foreign eyes.

3887
05:16:49,725 --> 05:16:55,542
He could feel them now. Not literally — the System
had given him many things, but not

3888
05:16:55,562 --> 05:17:01,445
that. But he could feel the shape of them, in the
Memory Palace, in the way the pieces

3889
05:17:01,466 --> 05:17:07,557
were arranging themselves on the table of his mind. A
patient Englishman. A careful woman

3890
05:17:07,577 --> 05:17:13,530
in Beijing. An angry colonel in Moscow. And, on his
own side of the ocean, an FBI agent

3891
05:17:13,550 --> 05:17:19,435
who had been reassigned to hazmat drills and had not
stopped, and a journalist who had

3892
05:17:19,455 --> 05:17:25,064
stopped writing and had not stopped listening, and
a doctor with an unread report.

3893
05:17:25,756 --> 05:17:27,121
They are gathering.

3894
05:17:27,641 --> 05:17:29,877
They're noticing. That isn't
the same thing.

3895
05:17:30,397 --> 05:17:36,584
It is how it starts. I told you. Two people in a
room. Then five. Then nations.

3896
05:17:38,105 --> 05:17:41,197
Then let's give the nations something
else to think about.

3897
05:17:43,217 --> 05:17:49,061
He did not say what he meant. The Adolf half of the
mind did not ask. It was still quiet,

3898
05:17:49,080 --> 05:17:52,488
the way it had been at the window
in Downing Street.

3899
05:17:53,058 --> 05:17:59,109
But on the table in the Memory Palace, in a room on
the fourth floor with no name on the

3900
05:18:16,565 --> 05:18:22,549
By the spring of twenty thirty-one, Owen Carrick had
become the thing that every institution

3901
05:18:22,569 --> 05:18:28,359
fears most in one of its own: a man who is right, and
cannot prove it, and will not stop.

3902
05:18:28,927 --> 05:18:34,796
He did the hazmat drills. He gave the chemistry
lectures. He handled the man in Scranton,

3903
05:18:34,816 --> 05:18:40,619
who had moved on to trying to buy fentanyl precursors
on the internet. And at night, and

3904
05:18:40,639 --> 05:18:46,441
on weekends, and on the vacation days he had been
saving for a trip to Disney World that

3905
05:18:46,461 --> 05:18:50,080
his daughters no longer mentioned,
he watched Umbrella.

3906
05:18:50,650 --> 05:18:55,376
It was Samira Haddad who found the way
in. Not a door. The air.

3907
05:18:55,945 --> 05:19:01,643
A laboratory of the highest containment grade breathes
in a particular way. It keeps itself

3908
05:19:01,663 --> 05:19:07,109
at negative pressure, so that nothing inside can
leak out; it pulls its exhaust through

3909
05:19:07,129 --> 05:19:12,449
two stages of very fine filters, and it pushes that
exhaust out through a stack, high

3910
05:19:12,469 --> 05:19:18,040
above the ground, at a steady rate that never varies,
day or night, weekends or holidays.

3911
05:19:18,060 --> 05:19:23,380
It is the heartbeat of the thing. You cannot hide
it, because if you ever stopped it,

3912
05:19:23,400 --> 05:19:25,515
whatever was inside would get out.

3913
05:19:26,205 --> 05:19:31,049
The old building has four exhaust stacks on the
roof. Three are what you'd expect for

3914
05:19:31,069 --> 05:19:35,914
a building that size. The fourth is too big. It
runs twenty-four hours a day. I had a

3915
05:19:35,934 --> 05:19:40,834
colleague look at the thermal imagery from the
state's aerial survey. It's warmer than

3916
05:19:40,854 --> 05:19:43,409
the others, and steadier, and
it never stops.

3917
05:19:43,929 --> 05:19:45,387
And if we could sample it?

3918
05:19:45,907 --> 05:19:50,585
Then I could tell you whether there's a grade-four
lab under that building. I couldn't

3919
05:19:50,605 --> 05:19:53,372
tell you what's in it. But I could
prove it exists.

3920
05:19:55,393 --> 05:20:01,905
It was, all three of them knew, a crime. Trespass,
at the least. For an FBI agent, the

3921
05:20:01,925 --> 05:20:05,325
end of a career. For a CDC
officer, the same.

3922
05:20:05,895 --> 05:20:11,332
They did it anyway, on the night of the eleventh of
April, because they had run out of

3923
05:20:11,352 --> 05:20:12,792
legal ways to be right.

3924
05:20:14,863 --> 05:20:20,638
They went up the fire escape at the back of the old
building at twenty to two in the morning.

3925
05:20:20,658 --> 05:20:25,998
Haddad carried a portable air sampler in a backpack.
Carrick carried his Bureau-issued

3926
05:20:26,019 --> 05:20:31,483
pistol, which he was not supposed to have on him
outside of duty hours, and which he had

3927
05:20:31,503 --> 05:20:34,412
brought without quite admitting
to himself why.

3928
05:20:34,983 --> 05:20:36,364
Nobody stopped them.

3929
05:20:36,935 --> 05:20:42,288
That should have told them something. Afterwards,
it did. There were six men in black

3930
05:20:42,308 --> 05:20:47,977
on the Umbrella campus that night. There were cameras
on every corner of the old building.

3931
05:20:47,997 --> 05:20:53,416
And two amateurs with a backpack climbed a fire
escape in plain sight, and reached the

3932
05:20:53,436 --> 05:20:55,121
roof, and nothing happened.

3933
05:20:55,691 --> 05:21:01,092
Haddad went straight to the fourth stack and set up
the sampler. It took nine minutes.

3934
05:21:01,112 --> 05:21:06,449
Carrick stood watch at the edge of the roof with
his back to her, looking down at the

3935
05:21:06,469 --> 05:21:11,741
empty parking lot where frame one thousand one
hundred and fourteen had been filmed.

3936
05:21:12,432 --> 05:21:18,852
Owen. Owen, it's grade four. Double high-efficiency
filtration. Negative pressure differential,

3937
05:21:18,872 --> 05:21:24,752
constant. There's a maximum-containment laboratory
under this building. It's real. It's

3938
05:21:24,772 --> 05:21:24,818
—

3939
05:21:26,340 --> 05:21:31,844
She stopped, because Carrick had turned around, and
was not looking at her. He was looking

3940
05:21:31,864 --> 05:21:34,912
past her, at the roof access door,
which was open.

3941
05:21:35,484 --> 05:21:41,312
It had been closed when they arrived. He was certain
of it. And now it was open, and the

3942
05:21:41,332 --> 05:21:47,163
stairwell behind it was lit, and at the top of the
stairs a stocky man in navy coveralls

3943
05:21:47,183 --> 05:21:51,485
with a tool belt was standing, perfectly
still, holding the door.

3944
05:21:52,173 --> 05:21:57,794
He says you can come down if you like, Agent
Carrick. Just you. Doctor Haddad can stay

3945
05:21:57,814 --> 05:21:58,908
with her machine.

3946
05:22:01,430 --> 05:22:06,128
He went. Of course he went. He had been
waiting two years to be invited.

3947
05:22:06,698 --> 05:22:12,932
In the freight elevator, standing beside Gus, he looked
at the panel. Loading dock. Basement.

3948
05:22:12,952 --> 05:22:18,916
And below them, where there had always been a blank
steel plate with four screws, a black

3949
05:22:18,936 --> 05:22:24,495
button with a thin crimson ring, glowing softly,
as if it had been there all along.

3950
05:22:25,066 --> 05:22:26,216
Gus pressed it.

3951
05:22:28,283 --> 05:22:30,257
The doors opened onto white.

3952
05:22:30,827 --> 05:22:36,226
He walked the corridor. He could not have told you
afterwards how long it took. He looked

3953
05:22:36,246 --> 05:22:41,462
into the laboratories through the glass, and saw
benches, and sequencers, and pressure

3954
05:22:41,483 --> 05:22:46,637
suits hanging like skins, and people in white coats
working at two in the morning who

3955
05:22:46,657 --> 05:22:51,875
did not look up at him as he passed. He saw an
enclosure behind thick glass where five

3956
05:22:51,895 --> 05:22:57,111
rhesus macaques sat very still, in a row, watching
him with eyes that caught the light

3957
05:22:57,131 --> 05:22:59,608
a little too brightly, and did not blink.

3958
05:23:00,177 --> 05:23:06,217
And at the end of the corridor, in front of a steel door
six inches thick, with a red-and-white

3959
05:23:06,237 --> 05:23:09,724
emblem painted on it, the Director
was waiting for him.

3960
05:23:11,915 --> 05:23:14,262
Hello, Owen. I thought it was
time you saw it.

3961
05:23:15,782 --> 05:23:22,087
He opened the steel door with a touch. Behind it, in a
cold room, stood a cryogenic cradle

3962
05:23:22,108 --> 05:23:28,202
nineteen feet tall. The produce truck had made its
long journey in reverse in February,

3963
05:23:28,222 --> 05:23:34,036
from the desert back to Delaware, and the grey
figure had been home for two months.

3964
05:23:34,604 --> 05:23:40,166
The frost cleared from the window, from the centre
outward. And Owen Carrick saw what

3965
05:23:40,186 --> 05:23:45,877
Rick Dorsey had seen, and Howard Lessing had heard,
and twelve Russian soldiers had met

3966
05:23:45,897 --> 05:23:47,322
in the dark in Nevada.

3967
05:23:47,891 --> 05:23:53,720
He did not scream. He did not run. He stood very
still for a long time, and he looked

3968
05:23:53,740 --> 05:23:59,568
at it, and the only sound in the cold room was the
slow breath of the compressor, and

3969
05:23:59,588 --> 05:24:00,532
his own heart.

3970
05:24:03,222 --> 05:24:04,215
What is it?

3971
05:24:04,733 --> 05:24:07,549
The reason nobody is ever going
to believe you.

3972
05:24:09,572 --> 05:24:11,034
Carrick drew his pistol.

3973
05:24:11,604 --> 05:24:17,083
He did not decide to. It happened the way things
happen when a mind has been stretched

3974
05:24:17,103 --> 05:24:22,583
too far for too long. He turned, and the weapon was
in his hand, and it was pointed at

3975
05:24:22,603 --> 05:24:28,403
the centre of the Director's chest from eight feet
away, and his finger was on the trigger.

3976
05:24:29,093 --> 05:24:30,370
You're under arrest.

3977
05:24:30,890 --> 05:24:31,596
For what?

3978
05:24:32,115 --> 05:24:35,040
I don't know. I don't care. I'll
think of something.

3979
05:24:36,560 --> 05:24:37,500
Go on, then.

3980
05:24:39,519 --> 05:24:40,568
He fired.

3981
05:24:41,138 --> 05:24:47,650
He fired fifteen times, until the slide locked back on
an empty magazine. He had qualified

3982
05:24:47,669 --> 05:24:53,888
as an expert marksman every year for thirteen years.
At eight feet, he could not miss.

3983
05:24:56,458 --> 05:25:01,884
When the smoke cleared, the Director was standing
exactly where he had been standing.

3984
05:25:01,904 --> 05:25:05,344
His coat was not marked. His
sunglasses were still on.

3985
05:25:05,915 --> 05:25:11,301
He held out his left hand, palm up. In it were
fifteen flattened pieces of lead.

3986
05:25:13,369 --> 05:25:18,900
He stepped forward, took Owen Carrick's right hand —
gently, the way you might take the

3987
05:25:18,920 --> 05:25:24,579
hand of a man who has fallen — turned it over, and
poured the bullets into his palm. They

3988
05:25:24,598 --> 05:25:25,216
were warm.

3989
05:25:25,905 --> 05:25:28,362
Keep those. You'll want something
to show people.

3990
05:25:30,882 --> 05:25:37,076
Carrick looked down at them. Fifteen pieces of lead
in his palm, warm, flattened like

3991
05:25:37,096 --> 05:25:42,778
coins. Behind him, in the frosted window, the
grey giant had not even stirred.

3992
05:25:44,968 --> 05:25:49,055
Let me tell you what happens now, Owen. Gus is going
to take you back up. You'll collect

3993
05:25:49,075 --> 05:25:53,254
Doctor Haddad from the roof. You'll go home to Laura.
And tomorrow morning at nine, you'll

3994
05:25:53,274 --> 05:25:57,500
learn that the Bureau's Office of Professional
Responsibility received, overnight, security

3995
05:25:57,520 --> 05:26:01,467
footage of you breaking into a private research
facility with a loaded service weapon

3996
05:26:01,487 --> 05:26:04,778
— footage in which, unlike some of my staff,
you are perfectly visible.

3997
05:26:05,180 --> 05:26:10,471
They'll also receive bank records. A numbered
account in Cyprus. The same bank Howard

3998
05:26:10,490 --> 05:26:16,032
Lessing used, when Halvorsen Pike paid him to let
four armed men into this building. Four

3999
05:26:16,052 --> 05:26:21,406
hundred thousand dollars, deposited in your name, in
twenty twenty-nine, the month you

4000
05:26:21,426 --> 05:26:22,845
opened him as a source.

4001
05:26:23,365 --> 05:26:24,409
I never —

4002
05:26:24,930 --> 05:26:29,771
I know. It won't matter. The Bureau will open an
investigation. It will take six weeks.

4003
05:26:29,791 --> 05:26:34,520
You'll be dismissed. No charges will be filed,
because Umbrella will decline to press

4004
05:26:34,540 --> 05:26:39,381
any, out of compassion for your family. It will be
in the papers. It will be a very sad

4005
05:26:39,400 --> 05:26:44,131
story, about a good agent who got too close to a
corrupt informant, and lost his way.

4006
05:26:46,150 --> 05:26:47,552
Why not just kill me?

4007
05:26:49,070 --> 05:26:54,934
Because dead, you're a martyr. People build things
on martyrs. Ruined, you're a madman

4008
05:26:54,954 --> 05:27:00,816
with a handful of bullets and a story about a
monster in a basement. Nobody listens to

4009
05:27:00,836 --> 05:27:01,294
madmen.

4010
05:27:03,314 --> 05:27:09,037
In the quiet of one skull, the old voice spoke —
very softly, and with something in it

4011
05:27:09,057 --> 05:27:10,772
that was almost amusement.

4012
05:27:11,463 --> 05:27:13,427
They listened to me.

4013
05:27:14,948 --> 05:27:20,868
Wesker did not answer it. Not aloud. But for an
instant, standing in the white corridor

4014
05:27:20,888 --> 05:27:26,879
with the ruined agent in front of him, he felt
something he had not felt in a long time,

4015
05:27:26,899 --> 05:27:28,040
and did not like.

4016
05:27:28,608 --> 05:27:29,741
It was doubt.

4017
05:27:31,931 --> 05:27:34,598
Goodbye, Owen. Give my regards to
your daughters.

4018
05:27:37,118 --> 05:27:41,581
It happened exactly as he had said. It
took six weeks, not seven.

4019
05:27:42,152 --> 05:27:48,175
The footage was clear. The bank records were
impeccable — impeccably forged, although

4020
05:27:48,195 --> 05:27:54,788
no forensic accountant the Bureau employed could ever
demonstrate how. Janet Kowal testified,

4021
05:27:54,808 --> 05:28:00,829
with visible pain, that Special Agent Carrick had
been reassigned for his own welfare

4022
05:28:00,849 --> 05:28:07,156
and had disregarded that reassignment. Samira Haddad,
suspended by the CDC the same week,

4023
05:28:07,176 --> 05:28:10,356
refused to testify against him,
and resigned.

4024
05:28:10,924 --> 05:28:17,153
Owen Carrick was dismissed from the Federal Bureau of
Investigation on the twenty-second

4025
05:28:17,173 --> 05:28:23,331
of May, twenty thirty-one, after seventeen years of
service, for conduct unbecoming. No

4026
05:28:23,351 --> 05:28:29,722
criminal charges were filed. Umbrella Life Sciences
issued a statement expressing sympathy

4027
05:28:29,742 --> 05:28:30,789
for his family.

4028
05:28:31,358 --> 05:28:37,220
He kept the fifteen bullets in a jam jar on the
kitchen windowsill. He showed them, over

4029
05:28:37,239 --> 05:28:42,967
the following year, to four journalists, two members
of Congress, a Pentagon official,

4030
05:28:42,987 --> 05:28:48,648
and a priest. Every one of them was very kind to
him, and every one of them went away

4031
05:28:48,668 --> 05:28:49,717
and did nothing.

4032
05:28:51,906 --> 05:28:53,146
Are you going to stop?

4033
05:28:55,166 --> 05:28:55,823
No.

4034
05:28:57,343 --> 05:29:00,949
Then I need you to tell me what that
means. For us. For the girls.

4035
05:29:02,969 --> 05:29:08,366
He did not have an answer. He would not have one
for a long time. Laura Carrick would

4036
05:29:08,386 --> 05:29:14,421
stay with him for three more years, out of love, and
then leave, out of love for her daughters.

4037
05:29:14,441 --> 05:29:18,373
She would never stop believing him. That
was the hardest part.

4038
05:29:21,193 --> 05:29:23,907
Assessment. Adversary broken.

4039
05:29:27,794 --> 05:29:33,835
The System called him broken. It was the System's
first real mistake about a human being.

4040
05:29:34,405 --> 05:29:40,266
Owen Carrick had been broken, it was true. And a man
who is broken does not always stay

4041
05:29:40,287 --> 05:29:46,217
in pieces. Sometimes — when the pieces are sharp
enough, and the reason is simple enough

4042
05:29:46,237 --> 05:29:52,098
— he puts himself back together around a single
idea, and becomes something that cannot

4043
05:29:52,118 --> 05:29:57,169
be broken again, because there is nothing left
in him that is not the idea.

4044
05:29:57,739 --> 05:30:03,494
He had lost his badge, his career, his pension, and,
in time, his marriage. He had kept

4045
05:30:03,514 --> 05:30:05,486
fifteen bullets and the truth.

4046
05:30:22,051 --> 05:30:27,970
Martin Sloane had waited nine months to meet the
thing in the satellite image. He was

4047
05:30:27,990 --> 05:30:33,559
a patient man. He had once waited four years in
Tbilisi for a single phone call.

4048
05:30:34,129 --> 05:30:39,771
They met in June, in the private room of a club in
Georgetown that did not have a name

4049
05:30:39,791 --> 05:30:45,367
on its door, at a table by a fire that was lit even
though it was summer, because the

4050
05:30:45,386 --> 05:30:51,095
club had been lighting it every evening since
nineteen fifty-three and saw no reason to

4051
05:30:51,115 --> 05:30:51,423
stop.

4052
05:30:52,115 --> 05:30:53,310
You know what I saw.

4053
05:30:53,831 --> 05:30:59,355
A satellite passed over Nevada in November. I
assume someone at Langley was awake.

4054
05:30:59,877 --> 05:31:05,370
Someone at Langley was very awake. Twelve heat
signatures in. One out. And a helicopter

4055
05:31:05,390 --> 05:31:09,237
that's now, I'm told, a very popular
new resident of Sinaloa.

4056
05:31:10,636 --> 05:31:14,837
I'm not going to ask you what it was. I'm not going
to ask you what else you have. I'm

4057
05:31:14,858 --> 05:31:19,157
going to tell you what I want, and then I'm going to
tell you what I'll give you for it,

4058
05:31:19,177 --> 05:31:20,727
and then you're going to decide.

4059
05:31:21,247 --> 05:31:22,336
That's refreshing.

4060
05:31:22,858 --> 05:31:25,226
I'm told you like people who don't
waste your time.

4061
05:31:27,128 --> 05:31:32,120
What I want is simple. There are things the United
States government would like to be

4062
05:31:32,140 --> 05:31:37,547
able to do, in the world, that it cannot do with its
own hands. Biological things. Defensive

4063
05:31:37,566 --> 05:31:42,737
things, mostly. Detection. Countermeasures. Vaccines
that exist before the disease does.

4064
05:31:42,757 --> 05:31:47,750
And, occasionally, other things. The kind of work
that, if a government did it, would

4065
05:31:47,770 --> 05:31:52,763
require a signature from someone elected. The kind
of work that, if a private company

4066
05:31:52,783 --> 05:31:56,479
did it, under contract, would require
only a signature from me.

4067
05:31:56,998 --> 05:31:58,101
Black contracts.

4068
05:31:58,619 --> 05:32:03,122
I prefer the term classified procurement. It
sounds so much less like a film.

4069
05:32:04,644 --> 05:32:05,711
And in exchange?

4070
05:32:06,230 --> 05:32:11,583
In exchange, the investigations stop. Not the Bureau —
the Bureau's already stopped, you've

4071
05:32:11,602 --> 05:32:16,777
seen to that. I mean the others. The ones you haven't
met yet. The Department of Defense

4072
05:32:16,797 --> 05:32:21,795
has a unit that watches for technological surprise.
So does the Department of Energy.

4073
05:32:21,815 --> 05:32:26,812
So does a very small office at the National
Security Council whose director reads the

4074
05:32:26,832 --> 05:32:32,420
same German patent reports that Julian Ashcombe does.
They're all going to find you eventually.

4075
05:32:32,440 --> 05:32:37,791
I can make sure they find you as a contractor. A
classified one. And classified contractors

4076
05:32:37,811 --> 05:32:40,153
are not investigated. They're protected.

4077
05:32:42,172 --> 05:32:47,608
The Director was silent for a while. He looked at
the fire. The Cold-Read had already

4078
05:32:47,628 --> 05:32:53,319
told him everything about Martin Sloane there was to
know: that he was telling the truth,

4079
05:32:53,340 --> 05:32:59,032
that he was not telling all of it, and that he
intended, in the long run, to own Umbrella

4080
05:32:59,052 --> 05:33:04,616
the way he owned every asset he had ever run — as a
tool, to be used and then put away.

4081
05:33:05,186 --> 05:33:10,070
That was fine. The Director intended exactly
the same thing in reverse.

4082
05:33:11,761 --> 05:33:13,972
He thinks he is buying you.

4083
05:33:14,494 --> 05:33:17,759
He is buying me. He just doesn't know
what he's paying with.

4084
05:33:18,279 --> 05:33:20,350
And what is he paying with?

4085
05:33:20,868 --> 05:33:26,954
Access. To every secret his government has about
biology. Every programme. Every laboratory.

4086
05:33:26,974 --> 05:33:32,992
Every weakness. He's going to open the door to the
house and invite me to fix the plumbing.

4087
05:33:34,891 --> 05:33:40,435
I'll need the first contract to be real, Mister
Sloane. Not a test. Something that matters.

4088
05:33:40,954 --> 05:33:41,802
It will be.

4089
05:33:42,325 --> 05:33:46,440
And I'll need you to understand that I don't
do anything I can't finish.

4090
05:33:46,959 --> 05:33:50,844
I watched the satellite, Doctor Wesker. I
understand that very well.

4091
05:33:52,864 --> 05:33:59,170
They shook hands over the fire. Sloane's grip was dry
and firm and did not hold on a moment

4092
05:33:59,190 --> 05:34:04,661
too long, and the Director, for once, did not
hold on a moment too long either.

4093
05:34:05,231 --> 05:34:11,841
It was, a historian would write decades later — in a
world that had stopped allowing historians

4094
05:34:11,861 --> 05:34:18,050
to write very much — the single most consequential
handshake of the twenty-first century.

4095
05:34:18,070 --> 05:34:22,726
At the time, it was two men in a private
club, agreeing on a price.

4096
05:34:25,296 --> 05:34:30,980
There was one more thing that had to be handled
before the handshake in Georgetown could

4097
05:34:31,001 --> 05:34:34,870
mean anything, and it was not an
agency. It was a committee.

4098
05:34:35,440 --> 05:34:41,133
In March of twenty thirty-one, the Senate Health
Committee opened hearings into the pricing

4099
05:34:41,153 --> 05:34:46,656
and supply of Aeterna. It was not a hostile
committee. Nine of its seventeen members had

4100
05:34:46,676 --> 05:34:52,181
constituents who credited the drug with a parent's
mobility. But the chair was a senator

4101
05:34:52,201 --> 05:34:57,705
from a state with no Umbrella facilities, who had
spent four years being told by her own

4102
05:34:57,725 --> 05:35:03,167
hospitals that they could not afford the one
medicine everybody wanted, and she was not

4103
05:35:03,187 --> 05:35:04,923
interested in being charmed.

4104
05:35:05,494 --> 05:35:11,316
Umbrella sent Catherine Adeyemi, not the Director.
That was deliberate and it was her

4105
05:35:11,336 --> 05:35:16,747
idea, and it was the single most effective
decision the company made that year.

4106
05:35:17,438 --> 05:35:22,104
